ForgeData.register("hist",{"HIST-BRIT1":{"label":"Britain 1906–1918","color":"#92400e","questions":[{"id":"HIST-BRIT1-01","spec":"AQA-2M-BRIT1","stem":"What does Liberal landslide of 1906 refer to?","options":{"A":"A Liberal landslide after Conservative splits over tariffs","B":"A campaign for imperial preference and protection rather than unrestricted free trade","C":"The war brought social and political change but imposed heavy economic and human costs","D":"A Liberal approach that accepted greater state action to address poverty and social risks"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-HIST-BRIT1-1","scaffold":"Liberal landslide of 1906: A Liberal landslide after Conservative splits over tariffs. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"Which example demonstrates Liberal landslide of 1906?","options":{"A":"It divided Conservatives and helped the Liberals present free trade as a popular economic interest","B":"A balanced judgement must compare state-building and reform with debt, loss and post-war instability","C":"The result gave Campbell-Bannerman's government room to pursue welfare and constitutional reform","D":"It provided the political framework for reforms such as school meals, pensions and national insurance"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-2m-1"},{"id":"HIST-BRIT1-02","spec":"AQA-2M-BRIT1","stem":"Which feature is most closely associated with Joseph Chamberlain's tariff reform?","options":{"A":"A Liberal approach that accepted greater state action to address poverty and social risks","B":"A campaign for imperial preference and protection rather than unrestricted free trade","C":"A Liberal landslide after Conservative splits over tariffs","D":"Lloyd George's budget that raised revenue through progressive taxation and land-related measures"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-HIST-BRIT1-2","scaffold":"Joseph Chamberlain's tariff reform: A campaign for imperial preference and protection rather than unrestricted free trade. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"A student applies Joseph Chamberlain's tariff reform to a case. Which judgement is strongest?","options":{"A":"It marked a major extension of state responsibility while leaving important groups outside coverage","B":"The result gave Campbell-Bannerman's government room to pursue welfare and constitutional reform","C":"It provided the political framework for reforms such as school meals, pensions and national insurance","D":"It divided Conservatives and helped the Liberals present free trade as a popular economic interest"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-2m-1"},{"id":"HIST-BRIT1-03","spec":"AQA-2M-BRIT1","stem":"On what grounds did New Liberals argue that state intervention was consistent with liberalism?","options":{"A":"They accepted that liberalism had failed and adopted socialist arguments instead","B":"They argued the state should own industry so that workers controlled their own labour","C":"They held that real freedom needs means the state can supply","D":"They claimed intervention was a temporary measure to be withdrawn once poverty fell"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-HIST-NEWLIB","scaffold":"Hobhouse and Hobson redefined liberty as POSITIVE freedom: a person who is starving, sick or illiterate is not meaningfully free, so the state must supply the conditions in which individual freedom can be exercised. That is an argument for liberalism's own goal by a different route, not a departure from it. Note the evidence: the 1911 Act was contributory, preserving self-help, and the Liberals nationalised nothing.","reforge":{"stem":"Which piece of evidence best refutes the claim that New Liberalism was simply socialism?","options":{"A":"National Insurance was contributory, preserving self-help","B":"The Liberals won a landslide in 1906 on a platform of free trade","C":"Labour remained a small party with fewer than fifty MPs before 1914","D":"Lloyd George's budget raised taxes on land and high incomes"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-2m-1"},{"id":"HIST-BRIT1-04","spec":"AQA-2M-BRIT1","stem":"How should 1902 Education Act be defined in this course?","options":{"A":"Checks on children's health carried out through the school system","B":"The provision of meals for children whose families could not afford adequate food","C":"A Liberal approach that accepted greater state action to address poverty and social risks","D":"It replaced school boards with local education authorities"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-HIST-BRIT1-4","scaffold":"1902 Education Act: It replaced school boards with local education authorities. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"How should 1902 Education Act be evaluated?","options":{"A":"It provided the political framework for reforms such as school meals, pensions and national insurance","B":"It strengthened central direction and caused tension with Nonconformist Liberal voters","C":"It addressed physical welfare and helped make poverty a matter of public policy","D":"It offered limited security to elderly people but excluded many and remained modest in value"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-2m-1"},{"id":"HIST-BRIT1-05","spec":"AQA-2M-BRIT1","stem":"By 1914, which group of people was left with no provision at all by the Liberal welfare reforms?","options":{"A":"Married women at home and workers in trades outside the insurance schemes","B":"Children in state elementary schools, who received neither meals nor inspection","C":"People aged over seventy, who were excluded from the pension on income grounds","D":"Manual workers in shipbuilding and engineering, who fell outside every scheme"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-HIST-LIB-WELFARE","scaffold":"The reforms were SELECTIVE, not universal. School meals (1906) and medical inspections (1907) covered children; the 1908 pension covered the over-seventies; the 1911 Act covered insured workers in named trades. Dependants were not covered by health insurance, and unemployment insurance reached only about 2.25 million men in cyclical trades. Calling 1906–14 'the creation of the welfare state' needs that qualification — universal provision arrives in 1945–48.","reforge":{"stem":"A student writes that the Liberal reforms 'created a welfare state covering everyone from cradle to grave'. Which correction is strongest?","options":{"A":"The reforms covered everyone but paid benefits too small to matter in practice","B":"The phrase belongs to Beveridge in 1942 and the reforms were aimed at particular groups","C":"Coverage targeted children, the elderly and insured workers, not most adults","D":"The reforms were universal in design but were never actually implemented before 1914"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-2m-1"},{"id":"HIST-BRIT1-06","spec":"AQA-2M-BRIT1","stem":"What role does medical inspections play in the topic?","options":{"A":"A non-contributory pension for many people aged over seventy","B":"Checks on children's health carried out through the school system","C":"The provision of meals for children whose families could not afford adequate food","D":"The creation of state-supported offices to match job seekers with vacancies"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-HIST-LIB-WELFARE","scaffold":"medical inspections: Checks on children's health carried out through the school system. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"In a comparison question, what should a student say about medical inspections?","options":{"A":"It financed welfare expansion and triggered a constitutional confrontation with the Lords","B":"It addressed physical welfare and helped make poverty a matter of public policy","C":"It offered limited security to elderly people but excluded many and remained modest in value","D":"They exposed poor health and created evidence supporting wider welfare intervention"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-2m-1"},{"id":"HIST-BRIT1-07","spec":"AQA-2M-BRIT1","stem":"How did the funding of the 1908 Old Age Pension differ from that of the 1911 National Insurance Act?","options":{"A":"Both were contributory, but the pension required a longer record of payments","B":"The pension came from taxation; the 1911 scheme was funded by contributions","C":"The pension was funded by employers; the 1911 scheme was funded by the state","D":"Both came from general taxation, but only the pension was subject to a means test"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-HIST-CONTRIB","scaffold":"The 1908 pension was NON-CONTRIBUTORY: paid from taxation, means-tested, 5s a week at seventy. The 1911 Act was CONTRIBUTORY and tripartite — 4d from the worker, 3d from the employer, 2d from the state, which Lloyd George sold as 'ninepence for fourpence'. The distinction matters politically: contribution made the benefit a right rather than charity and avoided the stigma of the Poor Law, but it excluded anyone who had not paid in.","reforge":{"stem":"Why did Lloyd George describe National Insurance as 'ninepence for fourpence'?","options":{"A":"The state added ninepence to every fourpence the worker paid into the scheme","B":"Benefits were worth ninepence a week against contributions of fourpence a week","C":"Employers paid ninepence for every fourpence contributed by their workforce","D":"The worker paid 4d, employer and state added 3d and 2d, making 9d in all"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-2m-1"},{"id":"HIST-BRIT1-08","spec":"AQA-2M-BRIT1","stem":"What is the supported by the evidence view of Labour Exchanges Act 1909?","options":{"A":"A contributory scheme providing health insurance and unemployment insurance for eligible workers","B":"Lloyd George's budget that raised revenue through progressive taxation and land-related measures","C":"A non-contributory pension for many people aged over seventy","D":"The creation of state-supported offices to match job seekers with vacancies"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-HIST-LIB-WELFARE","scaffold":"Labour Exchanges Act 1909: The creation of state-supported offices to match job seekers with vacancies. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"A historian or political analyst considers Labour Exchanges Act 1909. Which conclusion is sound?","options":{"A":"It offered limited security to elderly people but excluded many and remained modest in value","B":"It improved information in the labour market without guaranteeing employment","C":"It financed welfare expansion and triggered a constitutional confrontation with the Lords","D":"It marked a major extension of state responsibility while leaving important groups outside coverage"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-2m-1"},{"id":"HIST-BRIT1-09","spec":"AQA-2M-BRIT1","stem":"What was the causal relationship between the People's Budget and the Parliament Act?","options":{"A":"The Parliament Act was passed first, which then allowed the Budget to become law","B":"The two were unconnected: the Budget concerned taxation and the Act concerned Ireland","C":"The Lords passed the Budget, and the Act was later introduced to prevent future vetoes","D":"The Lords' rejection of the Budget triggered the crisis the Act was passed to settle"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-HIST-LORDS","scaffold":"Order: Budget introduced April 1909 → Lords reject it November 1909 → January 1910 election → Budget passes April 1910 → December 1910 election → Parliament Act August 1911. Rejecting a money bill broke a 250-year convention, turning a dispute about land taxes into a constitutional question about whether an unelected chamber could veto the elected one. The Act was the consequence, not the cause.","reforge":{"stem":"What did the Parliament Act 1911 actually do to the House of Lords?","options":{"A":"It abolished the Lords' power to reject or amend any legislation whatsoever","B":"It removed the veto on money bills and replaced the veto on other bills with a two-year delay","C":"It required the Lords to accept any bill twice approved by the electorate at a general election","D":"It reduced the number of hereditary peers entitled to vote on financial matters"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-2m-1"},{"id":"HIST-BRIT1-10","spec":"AQA-2M-BRIT1","stem":"Which distinction helps identify National Insurance Act 1911?","options":{"A":"Payments made by workers, employers and the state into the 1911 insurance schemes","B":"A contributory scheme providing health insurance and unemployment insurance for eligible workers","C":"Lloyd George's budget that raised revenue through progressive taxation and land-related measures","D":"Criticism that compulsory contributions and approved-society administration reduced union independence"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-HIST-CONTRIB","scaffold":"National Insurance Act 1911: A contributory scheme providing health insurance and unemployment insurance for eligible workers. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"Which interpretation of National Insurance Act 1911 is most accurate?","options":{"A":"A balanced judgement must compare state-building and reform with debt, loss and post-war instability","B":"It financed welfare expansion and triggered a constitutional confrontation with the Lords","C":"The tripartite structure shared cost but made benefits conditional on contribution","D":"It marked a major extension of state responsibility while leaving important groups outside coverage"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-2m-1"},{"id":"HIST-BRIT1-11","spec":"AQA-2M-BRIT1","stem":"A teacher asks for an accurate account of insurance contributions. Which answer works?","options":{"A":"A contributory scheme providing health insurance and unemployment insurance for eligible workers","B":"Criticism that compulsory contributions and approved-society administration reduced union independence","C":"Payments made by workers, employers and the state into the 1911 insurance schemes","D":"Legislation limiting the Lords' power to veto money bills and delaying other public bills"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-HIST-CONTRIB","scaffold":"insurance contributions: Payments made by workers, employers and the state into the 1911 insurance schemes. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"What does a case involving insurance contributions show?","options":{"A":"The tripartite structure shared cost but made benefits conditional on contribution","B":"It marked a major extension of state responsibility while leaving important groups outside coverage","C":"It showed that welfare reform could create conflict even among potential beneficiaries","D":"It turned a financial dispute into a constitutional struggle over the elected chamber's authority"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-2m-1"},{"id":"HIST-BRIT1-12","spec":"AQA-2M-BRIT1","stem":"Which consequence can be linked to trade union opposition to insurance?","options":{"A":"A reform that extended the vote to most men and some women over thirty meeting a property qualification","B":"The House of Lords rejected Lloyd George's 1909 budget","C":"Payments made by workers, employers and the state into the 1911 insurance schemes","D":"Criticism that compulsory contributions and approved-society administration reduced union independence"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-HIST-CONTRIB","scaffold":"trade union opposition to insurance: Criticism that compulsory contributions and approved-society administration reduced union independence. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"Which piece of evidence would best support an argument about trade union opposition to insurance?","options":{"A":"The tripartite structure shared cost but made benefits conditional on contribution","B":"It showed that welfare reform could create conflict even among potential beneficiaries","C":"It turned a financial dispute into a constitutional struggle over the elected chamber's authority","D":"It increased pressure on the British government and deepened the militarisation of the crisis"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-2m-1"},{"id":"HIST-BRIT1-13","spec":"AQA-2M-BRIT1","stem":"What does People's Budget rejection refer to?","options":{"A":"The House of Lords rejected Lloyd George's 1909 budget","B":"Legislation limiting the Lords' power to veto money bills and delaying other public bills","C":"Criticism that compulsory contributions and approved-society administration reduced union independence","D":"The political conflict over proposals for an Irish parliament within the United Kingdom"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-HIST-LORDS","scaffold":"People's Budget rejection: The House of Lords rejected Lloyd George's 1909 budget. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"Which example demonstrates People's Budget rejection?","options":{"A":"It reduced the upper chamber's ability to block elected-government programmes","B":"It showed that welfare reform could create conflict even among potential beneficiaries","C":"It turned a financial dispute into a constitutional struggle over the elected chamber's authority","D":"It made political citizenship a national issue but did not secure equal voting rights before 1918"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-2m-1"},{"id":"HIST-BRIT1-14","spec":"AQA-2M-BRIT1","stem":"Which feature is most closely associated with Parliament Act 1911?","options":{"A":"The political conflict over proposals for an Irish parliament within the United Kingdom","B":"Legislation limiting the Lords' power to veto money bills and delaying other public bills","C":"The House of Lords rejected Lloyd George's 1909 budget","D":"The 1912 proposal to create an Irish legislature while retaining Westminster responsibilities"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-HIST-LORDS","scaffold":"Parliament Act 1911: Legislation limiting the Lords' power to veto money bills and delaying other public bills. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"A student applies Parliament Act 1911 to a case. Which judgement is strongest?","options":{"A":"It intensified Unionist resistance and made Ireland central to British politics","B":"It turned a financial dispute into a constitutional struggle over the elected chamber's authority","C":"It threatened civil conflict and exposed the limits of parliamentary compromise","D":"It reduced the upper chamber's ability to block elected-government programmes"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-2m-1"},{"id":"HIST-BRIT1-15","spec":"AQA-2M-BRIT1","stem":"Why is the Home Rule crisis of 1912–14 treated as a crisis of BRITISH politics, not only Irish politics?","options":{"A":"The Liberal government lost its Commons majority over the issue in 1913","B":"Home Rule would have removed all Irish members from the Westminster Parliament","C":"The dispute delayed every other item of Liberal legislation until war broke out","D":"A party of government backed resistance and army obedience was in doubt"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-HIST-HOMERULE","scaffold":"Bonar Law told the 1912 Blenheim rally he could 'imagine no length of resistance' to which Ulster might go that he would not support — a party of government endorsing resistance to an Act of Parliament. At the Curragh in March 1914, around sixty officers indicated they would resign rather than coerce Ulster, putting the army's obedience to a Liberal cabinet in question. The constitutional order itself was in doubt.","reforge":{"stem":"What happened to the Third Home Rule Bill in September 1914?","options":{"A":"It was defeated in the Lords and abandoned by the government","B":"It reached the statute book but was immediately suspended for the war","C":"It was amended to exclude the six counties of Ulster permanently","D":"It was withdrawn by Asquith in exchange for Unionist support for the war"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-2m-1"},{"id":"HIST-BRIT1-16","spec":"AQA-2M-BRIT1","stem":"How should Third Home Rule Bill be defined in this course?","options":{"A":"Wartime legislation granting the government extensive powers over information, industry and behaviour","B":"A 1912 pledge by Ulster Unionists to resist Home Rule","C":"The political conflict over proposals for an Irish parliament within the United Kingdom","D":"The 1912 proposal to create an Irish legislature while retaining Westminster responsibilities"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-HIST-HOMERULE","scaffold":"Third Home Rule Bill: The 1912 proposal to create an Irish legislature while retaining Westminster responsibilities. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"How should Third Home Rule Bill be evaluated?","options":{"A":"It threatened civil conflict and exposed the limits of parliamentary compromise","B":"It intensified Unionist resistance and made Ireland central to British politics","C":"It showed organised opposition mobilising outside Parliament","D":"It increased pressure on the British government and deepened the militarisation of the crisis"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-2m-1"},{"id":"HIST-BRIT1-17","spec":"AQA-2M-BRIT1","stem":"Which example best illustrates Ulster Covenant?","options":{"A":"A 1912 pledge by Ulster Unionists to resist Home Rule","B":"A Unionist armed organisation formed to resist implementation of Home Rule","C":"The 1912 proposal to create an Irish legislature while retaining Westminster responsibilities","D":"A nationalist force created partly in response to the Ulster Volunteers"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-HIST-HOMERULE","scaffold":"Ulster Covenant: A 1912 pledge by Ulster Unionists to resist Home Rule. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"What is the defensible view of Ulster Covenant?","options":{"A":"Its existence made the possibility of armed confrontation more credible","B":"It intensified Unionist resistance and made Ireland central to British politics","C":"It showed organised opposition mobilising outside Parliament","D":"It increased pressure on the British government and deepened the militarisation of the crisis"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-2m-1"},{"id":"HIST-BRIT1-18","spec":"AQA-2M-BRIT1","stem":"What role does Ulster Volunteer Force play in the topic?","options":{"A":"A nationalist force created partly in response to the Ulster Volunteers","B":"A Unionist armed organisation formed to resist implementation of Home Rule","C":"A 1912 pledge by Ulster Unionists to resist Home Rule","D":"The 1914 refusal or threatened refusal of army officers to enforce Home Rule against Ulster"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-HIST-HOMERULE","scaffold":"Ulster Volunteer Force: A Unionist armed organisation formed to resist implementation of Home Rule. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"In a comparison question, what should a student say about Ulster Volunteer Force?","options":{"A":"It revealed the political sensitivity of using the army in a domestic constitutional dispute","B":"It showed organised opposition mobilising outside Parliament","C":"It increased pressure on the British government and deepened the militarisation of the crisis","D":"Its existence made the possibility of armed confrontation more credible"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-2m-1"},{"id":"HIST-BRIT1-19","spec":"AQA-2M-BRIT1","stem":"Why is Irish Volunteers significant?","options":{"A":"A Unionist armed organisation formed to resist implementation of Home Rule","B":"The 1914 refusal or threatened refusal of army officers to enforce Home Rule against Ulster","C":"A nationalist force created partly in response to the Ulster Volunteers","D":"A 1912 controversy over ministers' share dealings and alleged insider knowledge"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-HIST-HOMERULE","scaffold":"Irish Volunteers: A nationalist force created partly in response to the Ulster Volunteers. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"Which outcome is most closely connected with Irish Volunteers?","options":{"A":"It increased pressure on the British government and deepened the militarisation of the crisis","B":"Its existence made the possibility of armed confrontation more credible","C":"It revealed the political sensitivity of using the army in a domestic constitutional dispute","D":"It damaged Liberal credibility even though the ministers were not proven to have acted corruptly"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-2m-1"},{"id":"HIST-BRIT1-20","spec":"AQA-2M-BRIT1","stem":"What is the supported by the evidence view of Curragh incident?","options":{"A":"A government including leading Liberals and Conservatives formed during the First World War","B":"A 1912 controversy over ministers' share dealings and alleged insider knowledge","C":"A nationalist force created partly in response to the Ulster Volunteers","D":"The 1914 refusal or threatened refusal of army officers to enforce Home Rule against Ulster"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-HIST-HOMERULE","scaffold":"Curragh incident: The 1914 refusal or threatened refusal of army officers to enforce Home Rule against Ulster. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"A historian or political analyst considers Curragh incident. Which conclusion is sound?","options":{"A":"It increased pressure on the British government and deepened the militarisation of the crisis","B":"It revealed the political sensitivity of using the army in a domestic constitutional dispute","C":"It damaged Liberal credibility even though the ministers were not proven to have acted corruptly","D":"It made political citizenship a national issue but did not secure equal voting rights before 1918"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-2m-1"},{"id":"HIST-BRIT1-21","spec":"AQA-2M-BRIT1","stem":"How does Marconi scandal affect the system or period being studied?","options":{"A":"A 1912 controversy over ministers' share dealings and alleged insider knowledge","B":"The organised movement seeking votes for women through constitutional and militant methods","C":"The 1914 refusal or threatened refusal of army officers to enforce Home Rule against Ulster","D":"Tactics such as window-breaking, arson and hunger strikes used by parts of the WSPU"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-HIST-BRIT1-21","scaffold":"Marconi scandal: A 1912 controversy over ministers' share dealings and alleged insider knowledge. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"Why might Marconi scandal matter in this context?","options":{"A":"It made political citizenship a national issue but did not secure equal voting rights before 1918","B":"It revealed the political sensitivity of using the army in a domestic constitutional dispute","C":"It damaged Liberal credibility even though the ministers were not proven to have acted corruptly","D":"Its militant strategy kept suffrage visible but narrowed the movement's appeal to some observers"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-2m-1"},{"id":"HIST-BRIT1-22","spec":"AQA-2M-BRIT1","stem":"Which option correctly distinguishes the NUWSS from the WSPU?","options":{"A":"The NUWSS used constitutional methods and was far the larger of the two","B":"The WSPU used constitutional methods and was far the larger of the two","C":"Both used militant methods, but only the WSPU accepted male members","D":"The NUWSS was founded after the WSPU, in response to the failure of militancy"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-HIST-SUFFRAGE","scaffold":"NUWSS: founded 1897, led by Millicent Fawcett, constitutional — petitions, lobbying, supporting sympathetic candidates — with around 50,000 members by 1914. WSPU: founded 1903, led by Emmeline Pankhurst, militant, with roughly 2,000. The size comparison surprises students: the constitutional society was about twenty-five times larger. 'Suffragette' was coined as mockery by the Daily Mail in 1906; 'suffragist' is the older and broader term covering both.","reforge":{"stem":"Why does the distinction between suffragist and suffragette matter to a historian?","options":{"A":"The debate over whether militancy helped or harmed the cause needs them separated","B":"The terms describe different decades rather than different organisations","C":"Only suffragettes were prosecuted, so the court records describe one group","D":"Suffragists opposed votes for women, so conflating the two reverses their position"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-2m-1"},{"id":"HIST-BRIT1-23","spec":"AQA-2M-BRIT1","stem":"A teacher asks for an accurate account of suffragette militancy. Which answer works?","options":{"A":"The organised movement seeking votes for women through constitutional and militant methods","B":"The Women's Social and Political Union led by Emmeline Pankhurst","C":"Tactics such as window-breaking, arson and hunger strikes used by parts of the WSPU","D":"A government including leading Liberals and Conservatives formed during the First World War"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-HIST-SUFFRAGE","scaffold":"suffragette militancy: Tactics such as window-breaking, arson and hunger strikes used by parts of the WSPU. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"What does a case involving suffragette militancy show?","options":{"A":"It attracted publicity but also allowed opponents to portray the campaign as disorderly","B":"It made political citizenship a national issue but did not secure equal voting rights before 1918","C":"Its militant strategy kept suffrage visible but narrowed the movement's appeal to some observers","D":"It reduced normal party competition while concentrating authority in a national emergency"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-2m-1"},{"id":"HIST-BRIT1-24","spec":"AQA-2M-BRIT1","stem":"Which consequence can be linked to WSPU?","options":{"A":"A law allowing temporary release of hunger-striking prisoners before re-arrest","B":"The National Union of Women's Suffrage Societies led by Millicent Fawcett","C":"Tactics such as window-breaking, arson and hunger strikes used by parts of the WSPU","D":"The Women's Social and Political Union led by Emmeline Pankhurst"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-HIST-SUFFRAGE","scaffold":"WSPU: The Women's Social and Political Union led by Emmeline Pankhurst. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"Which piece of evidence would best support an argument about WSPU?","options":{"A":"It attracted publicity but also allowed opponents to portray the campaign as disorderly","B":"Its militant strategy kept suffrage visible but narrowed the movement's appeal to some observers","C":"Constitutional campaigning built a respectable mass movement","D":"It helped maintain army strength but challenged ideas of individual liberty and voluntary service"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-2m-1"},{"id":"HIST-BRIT1-25","spec":"AQA-2M-BRIT1","stem":"What does NUWSS refer to?","options":{"A":"The National Union of Women's Suffrage Societies led by Millicent Fawcett","B":"A law allowing temporary release of hunger-striking prisoners before re-arrest","C":"The Women's Social and Political Union led by Emmeline Pankhurst","D":"A government including leading Liberals and Conservatives formed during the First World War"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-HIST-SUFFRAGE","scaffold":"NUWSS: The National Union of Women's Suffrage Societies led by Millicent Fawcett. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"Which example demonstrates NUWSS?","options":{"A":"It showed the political cost of force-feeding","B":"Its militant strategy kept suffrage visible but narrowed the movement's appeal to some observers","C":"Constitutional campaigning built a respectable mass movement","D":"The war postponed the immediate Home Rule crisis and transformed the role of the state"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-2m-1"},{"id":"HIST-BRIT1-26","spec":"AQA-2M-BRIT1","stem":"Which feature is most closely associated with Cat and Mouse Act 1913?","options":{"A":"Britain entered the European conflict after Germany invaded Belgium","B":"A law allowing temporary release of hunger-striking prisoners before re-arrest","C":"The National Union of Women's Suffrage Societies led by Millicent Fawcett","D":"A government including leading Liberals and Conservatives formed during the First World War"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-HIST-SUFFRAGE","scaffold":"Cat and Mouse Act 1913: A law allowing temporary release of hunger-striking prisoners before re-arrest. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"A student applies Cat and Mouse Act 1913 to a case. Which judgement is strongest?","options":{"A":"It reduced normal party competition while concentrating authority in a national emergency","B":"Constitutional campaigning built a respectable mass movement","C":"The war postponed the immediate Home Rule crisis and transformed the role of the state","D":"It showed the political cost of force-feeding"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-2m-1"},{"id":"HIST-BRIT1-27","spec":"AQA-2M-BRIT1","stem":"A student is revising 1914 outbreak of war. Which description is accurate?","options":{"A":"A law allowing temporary release of hunger-striking prisoners before re-arrest","B":"A government including leading Liberals and Conservatives formed during the First World War","C":"Britain entered the European conflict after Germany invaded Belgium","D":"Wartime legislation granting the government extensive powers over information, industry and behaviour"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-HIST-BRIT1-27","scaffold":"1914 outbreak of war: Britain entered the European conflict after Germany invaded Belgium. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"What is the main significance of 1914 outbreak of war?","options":{"A":"The war postponed the immediate Home Rule crisis and transformed the role of the state","B":"It showed the political cost of force-feeding","C":"It reduced normal party competition while concentrating authority in a national emergency","D":"It helped maintain army strength but challenged ideas of individual liberty and voluntary service"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-2m-2"},{"id":"HIST-BRIT1-28","spec":"AQA-2M-BRIT1","stem":"How should wartime coalition be defined in this course?","options":{"A":"A reform that extended the vote to most men and some women over thirty meeting a property qualification","B":"Wartime legislation granting the government extensive powers over information, industry and behaviour","C":"Britain entered the European conflict after Germany invaded Belgium","D":"A government including leading Liberals and Conservatives formed during the First World War"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-HIST-BRIT1-28","scaffold":"wartime coalition: A government including leading Liberals and Conservatives formed during the First World War. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"How should wartime coalition be evaluated?","options":{"A":"The war postponed the immediate Home Rule crisis and transformed the role of the state","B":"It reduced normal party competition while concentrating authority in a national emergency","C":"It increased executive power and restricted civil liberties in the name of security","D":"It helped maintain army strength but challenged ideas of individual liberty and voluntary service"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-2m-2"},{"id":"HIST-BRIT1-29","spec":"AQA-2M-BRIT1","stem":"Which example best illustrates Defence of the Realm Act?","options":{"A":"Wartime legislation granting the government extensive powers over information, industry and behaviour","B":"The organisation of labour, industry, food and military manpower for total war","C":"A government including leading Liberals and Conservatives formed during the First World War","D":"A reform that extended the vote to most men and some women over thirty meeting a property qualification"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-HIST-BRIT1-29","scaffold":"Defence of the Realm Act: Wartime legislation granting the government extensive powers over information, industry and behaviour. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"What is the defensible view of Defence of the Realm Act?","options":{"A":"It expanded state intervention far beyond the pre-war Liberal programme","B":"It reduced normal party competition while concentrating authority in a national emergency","C":"It increased executive power and restricted civil liberties in the name of security","D":"It helped maintain army strength but challenged ideas of individual liberty and voluntary service"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-2m-2"},{"id":"HIST-BRIT1-30","spec":"AQA-2M-BRIT1","stem":"What role does wartime mobilisation play in the topic?","options":{"A":"Compulsory military service introduced during the First World War","B":"The organisation of labour, industry, food and military manpower for total war","C":"Wartime legislation granting the government extensive powers over information, industry and behaviour","D":"Women's increased employment in munitions, transport, agriculture and public services"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-HIST-BRIT1-30","scaffold":"wartime mobilisation: The organisation of labour, industry, food and military manpower for total war. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"In a comparison question, what should a student say about wartime mobilisation?","options":{"A":"It changed gender roles in practice but did not automatically produce equality after the war","B":"It increased executive power and restricted civil liberties in the name of security","C":"It helped maintain army strength but challenged ideas of individual liberty and voluntary service","D":"It expanded state intervention far beyond the pre-war Liberal programme"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-2m-2"},{"id":"HIST-BRIT1-31","spec":"AQA-2M-BRIT1","stem":"Why is conscription significant?","options":{"A":"The organisation of labour, industry, food and military manpower for total war","B":"Women's increased employment in munitions, transport, agriculture and public services","C":"Compulsory military service introduced during the First World War","D":"A reform that extended the vote to most men and some women over thirty meeting a property qualification"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-HIST-BRIT1-31","scaffold":"conscription: Compulsory military service introduced during the First World War. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"Which outcome is most closely connected with conscription?","options":{"A":"It helped maintain army strength but challenged ideas of individual liberty and voluntary service","B":"It expanded state intervention far beyond the pre-war Liberal programme","C":"It changed gender roles in practice but did not automatically produce equality after the war","D":"A balanced judgement must compare state-building and reform with debt, loss and post-war instability"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-2m-2"},{"id":"HIST-BRIT1-32","spec":"AQA-2M-BRIT1","stem":"What is the strongest objection to the claim that women's war work won them the vote in 1918?","options":{"A":"Women's war work was concentrated in munitions, which closed at the armistice","B":"Asquith remained opposed to women's suffrage throughout the war years","C":"Those enfranchised were mostly over thirty and propertied, not young war workers","D":"Parliament debated the franchise in 1916, before most women had entered war work"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-HIST-WARCAUSE","scaffold":"If war work were the cause, the Act enfranchised close to the wrong women: those over thirty with a property qualification, rather than the young single munitions workers whose service is being credited. The mechanism was procedural — soldiers had lost their residence qualification, so a franchise bill was needed regardless, and the Speaker's Conference of 1916–17 settled the terms. The pre-war NUWSS campaign had already built the majority. War was the occasion rather than the cause.","reforge":{"stem":"Why has the 'reward for war work' explanation proved so durable despite the evidence against it?","options":{"A":"It was the explanation given by the Act itself in its preamble","B":"No records of the pre-war suffrage campaign survived the war","C":"Militant suffragettes themselves promoted it after 1918 to justify their tactics","D":"It suited everyone: it credited women without conceding the campaign was right"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-2m-2"},{"id":"HIST-BRIT1-33","spec":"AQA-2M-BRIT1","stem":"Which women received the vote under the Representation of the People Act 1918?","options":{"A":"All women over twenty-one, on the same terms as men after the same Act","B":"Women over thirty meeting a property qualification, about 40% of adult women","C":"Women over thirty who had undertaken recognised war work in industry or nursing","D":"Women over twenty-five who were married to men on the parliamentary register"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-HIST-FRANCHISE","scaffold":"The Act enfranchised women over thirty who were householders, wives of householders, occupiers of property worth £5, or graduates — some 8.4 million, roughly 40% of adult women. All men over twenty-one got the vote (nineteen for servicemen). Equal franchise came in 1928. The age bar was deliberate arithmetic: after the war's casualties, enfranchising all adult women would have made women a majority of the electorate.","reforge":{"stem":"Why did the 1918 Act change the male franchise at the same time as the female one?","options":{"A":"Ministers wanted to conceal the extension to women inside a larger reform","B":"The Speaker's Conference had been instructed to consider only male voters","C":"Servicemen's residence qualifications had lapsed, so a bill was needed anyway","D":"Male suffrage had to be widened to keep women a minority of the total electorate"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-2m-2"},{"id":"HIST-BRIT1-34","spec":"AQA-2M-BRIT1","stem":"Which distinction helps identify 1918 general election?","options":{"A":"The war increased state capacity, debt, social expectations and imperial strain","B":"The first election held after the Representation of the People Act and the wartime coalition","C":"A reform that extended the vote to most men and some women over thirty meeting a property qualification","D":"The growth of Labour's electoral and organisational importance before and during the First World War"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-HIST-FRANCHISE","scaffold":"1918 general election: The first election held after the Representation of the People Act and the wartime coalition. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"Which interpretation of 1918 general election is most accurate?","options":{"A":"It improved provision but was limited by means tests, contribution rules and continuing poverty","B":"It transformed the electorate while stopping short of equal adult suffrage","C":"Its effects created both a stronger welfare consensus and serious post-war economic problems","D":"It confirmed the Conservatives' strength and marked the decline of the pre-war Liberal system"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-2m-2"},{"id":"HIST-BRIT1-35","spec":"AQA-2M-BRIT1","stem":"A teacher asks for an accurate account of impact of the First World War on Britain. Which answer works?","options":{"A":"The first election held after the Representation of the People Act and the wartime coalition","B":"The collapse of the Liberal Party as the main alternative to the Conservatives","C":"The war increased state capacity, debt, social expectations and imperial strain","D":"The growth of Labour's electoral and organisational importance before and during the First World War"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-HIST-BRIT1-35","scaffold":"impact of the First World War on Britain: The war increased state capacity, debt, social expectations and imperial strain. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"What does a case involving impact of the First World War on Britain show?","options":{"A":"Its effects created both a stronger welfare consensus and serious post-war economic problems","B":"It confirmed the Conservatives' strength and marked the decline of the pre-war Liberal system","C":"Wartime splits, leadership conflict and Labour's rise","D":"It improved provision but was limited by means tests, contribution rules and continuing poverty"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-2m-2"},{"id":"HIST-BRIT1-36","spec":"AQA-2M-BRIT1","stem":"Which consequence can be linked to Liberal decline?","options":{"A":"The war brought social and political change but imposed heavy economic and human costs","B":"The growth of Labour's electoral and organisational importance before and during the First World War","C":"The war increased state capacity, debt, social expectations and imperial strain","D":"The collapse of the Liberal Party as the main alternative to the Conservatives"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-HIST-BRIT1-36","scaffold":"Liberal decline: The collapse of the Liberal Party as the main alternative to the Conservatives. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"Which piece of evidence would best support an argument about Liberal decline?","options":{"A":"Its effects created both a stronger welfare consensus and serious post-war economic problems","B":"Wartime splits, leadership conflict and Labour's rise","C":"It was supported by trade-union links, expanded suffrage and the Liberals' loss of support","D":"It improved provision but was limited by means tests, contribution rules and continuing poverty"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-2m-2"},{"id":"HIST-BRIT1-37","spec":"AQA-2M-BRIT1","stem":"What does rise of Labour refer to?","options":{"A":"The growth of Labour's electoral and organisational importance before and during the First World War","B":"The Liberal programme of intervention before 1914","C":"The collapse of the Liberal Party as the main alternative to the Conservatives","D":"Criticism that compulsory contributions and approved-society administration reduced union independence"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-HIST-BRIT1-37","scaffold":"rise of Labour: The growth of Labour's electoral and organisational importance before and during the First World War. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"Which example demonstrates rise of Labour?","options":{"A":"It improved provision but was limited by means tests, contribution rules and continuing poverty","B":"Wartime splits, leadership conflict and Labour's rise","C":"It was supported by trade-union links, expanded suffrage and the Liberals' loss of support","D":"A balanced judgement must compare state-building and reform with debt, loss and post-war instability"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-2m-2"},{"id":"HIST-BRIT1-38","spec":"AQA-2M-BRIT1","stem":"Which feature is most closely associated with pre-war welfare reform?","options":{"A":"Surveys and reports documenting poverty and working-class living conditions","B":"The Liberal programme of intervention before 1914","C":"The growth of Labour's electoral and organisational importance before and during the First World War","D":"The system divided costs between workers, employers and the state"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-HIST-LIB-WELFARE","scaffold":"pre-war welfare reform: The Liberal programme of intervention before 1914. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"A student applies pre-war welfare reform to a case. Which judgement is strongest?","options":{"A":"A balanced judgement must compare state-building and reform with debt, loss and post-war instability","B":"It was supported by trade-union links, expanded suffrage and the Liberals' loss of support","C":"They showed poverty could be structural, not personal failure","D":"It improved provision but was limited by means tests, contribution rules and continuing poverty"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-2m-2"},{"id":"HIST-BRIT1-39","spec":"AQA-2M-BRIT1","stem":"A student is revising Liberal social investigation. Which description is accurate?","options":{"A":"The Liberal programme of intervention before 1914","B":"The system divided costs between workers, employers and the state","C":"Surveys and reports documenting poverty and working-class living conditions","D":"The war brought social and political change but imposed heavy economic and human costs"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-HIST-LIB-WELFARE","scaffold":"Liberal social investigation: Surveys and reports documenting poverty and working-class living conditions. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"What is the main significance of Liberal social investigation?","options":{"A":"They showed poverty could be structural, not personal failure","B":"It improved provision but was limited by means tests, contribution rules and continuing poverty","C":"It made welfare politically and financially sustainable but left coverage incomplete","D":"A balanced judgement must compare state-building and reform with debt, loss and post-war instability"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-2m-2"},{"id":"HIST-BRIT1-40","spec":"AQA-2M-BRIT1","stem":"How should National Insurance finance be defined in this course?","options":{"A":"A campaign for imperial preference and protection rather than unrestricted free trade","B":"The war brought social and political change but imposed heavy economic and human costs","C":"Surveys and reports documenting poverty and working-class living conditions","D":"The system divided costs between workers, employers and the state"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-HIST-CONTRIB","scaffold":"National Insurance finance: The system divided costs between workers, employers and the state. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"How should National Insurance finance be evaluated?","options":{"A":"They showed poverty could be structural, not personal failure","B":"It made welfare politically and financially sustainable but left coverage incomplete","C":"A Liberal landslide after Conservative splits over tariffs","D":"The result gave Campbell-Bannerman's government room to pursue welfare and constitutional reform"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-2m-2"},{"id":"HIST-BRIT1-41","spec":"AQA-2M-BRIT1","stem":"Which answer to 'How far did the First World War change Britain?' would reach the highest band?","options":{"A":"One that states a criterion for 'change' and reaches a verdict the evidence supports","B":"One that lists the major changes in each area of British life in chronological order","C":"One that describes both continuity and change in equal measure without concluding","D":"One that covers the greatest number of accurately dated developments and statistics"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-HIST-JUDGEMENT","scaffold":"Accurate narrative reaches the middle band; JUDGEMENT reaches the top. A judgement needs a stated criterion — change measured against what, and by when — a verdict, and evidence subordinated to the argument rather than listed before it. Balance without conclusion is not judgement either: 'there was both change and continuity' answers nothing. Detail earns marks only when it supports a claim.","reforge":{"stem":"A paragraph gives accurate dates and figures but reaches no verdict. What is the single most useful improvement?","options":{"A":"Add more supporting evidence to strengthen the factual foundation","B":"Remove the weaker examples so the strongest evidence stands out clearly","C":"Open with a claim the paragraph argues for, and state the criterion behind it","D":"Rearrange the material into strict chronological order so the trend is visible"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-2m-2"},{"id":"HIST-BRIT1-42","spec":"AQA-2M-BRIT1","tag":"MC-HIST-NEWLIB","specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-2m-1","stem":"Which policy would a New Liberal have been LEAST likely to support before 1914?","options":{"A":"A contributory insurance scheme covering sickness for workers in named trades","B":"Progressive taxation of high incomes and land values to fund social provision","C":"Public ownership of the major industries, run in the interest of the workforce","D":"State-funded school meals and medical inspection for elementary school children"},"correct":"C","scaffold":"New Liberalism accepted state action to create the conditions for individual freedom, but not common ownership. Hobhouse and Hobson argued for intervention WITHIN a market economy: contributory insurance preserved self-help, progressive taxation funded provision, and welfare measures targeted specific groups. Nationalisation belongs to the socialist tradition, and the Liberals nationalised nothing before 1914.","reforge":{"stem":"Which thinker is most associated with the New Liberal case for state intervention?","options":{"A":"L. T. Hobhouse, who argued freedom needs the means to exercise it","B":"Herbert Spencer, who applied evolutionary argument to social policy","C":"Sidney Webb, who set out the Fabian case for gradual collectivism","D":"William Beveridge, whose 1942 report defined the five giants"},"correct":"A"}},{"id":"HIST-BRIT1-43","spec":"AQA-2M-BRIT1","tag":"MC-HIST-WARCAUSE","specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-2m-2","stem":"Which factor made a franchise bill unavoidable by 1917, regardless of the argument over women's suffrage?","options":{"A":"The Parliament Act 1911 required a general election to be held by 1918","B":"The wartime coalition had promised a franchise bill in exchange for Labour support","C":"The pre-war suffrage campaign had secured a Commons majority that could not be ignored","D":"Millions of servicemen had lost the residence qualification needed to vote"},"correct":"D","scaffold":"Residence qualifications lapsed for men serving abroad, so the existing register would have disenfranchised the very men who had fought — politically impossible. A bill was therefore required whatever view was taken of women's suffrage, and once the franchise was being rewritten the women's claim could not be kept off the table. The Speaker's Conference of 1916–17 settled the terms. This is why the war is better described as the occasion than the cause.","reforge":{"stem":"What did the Speaker's Conference of 1916–17 do?","options":{"A":"It ruled that women's suffrage required a separate bill after the war","B":"It settled cross-party terms for franchise reform, including some women","C":"It recommended universal adult suffrage, which Parliament then rejected","D":"It investigated the conduct of militant suffragettes before the war"},"correct":"B"}}]},"HIST-BRIT2":{"label":"Britain 1918–1957","color":"#92400e","questions":[{"id":"HIST-BRIT2-01","spec":"AQA-2M-BRIT2","stem":"What does post-war unemployment refer to?","options":{"A":"High unemployment after demobilisation and the contraction of wartime industries","B":"Public spending cuts recommended in the early 1920s to reduce the budget deficit","C":"The period combined depression, war and reconstruction with major changes in state and society","D":"Coal owners reduced wages and extended working hours after the government subsidy ended"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-HIST-BRIT2-1","scaffold":"post-war unemployment: High unemployment after demobilisation and the contraction of wartime industries. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"Which example demonstrates post-war unemployment?","options":{"A":"It protected financial confidence but reduced demand and worsened the social impact of unemployment","B":"Weighing living standards, welfare and Britain's world role","C":"It made the return to peacetime difficult and strengthened demands for state protection","D":"Its recommendations were rejected by miners because they implied wage cuts and longer hours"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-2m-3"},{"id":"HIST-BRIT2-02","spec":"AQA-2M-BRIT2","stem":"Which feature is most closely associated with Geddes Axe?","options":{"A":"A nationwide stoppage called in support of locked-out coal miners","B":"Public spending cuts recommended in the early 1920s to reduce the budget deficit","C":"High unemployment after demobilisation and the contraction of wartime industries","D":"Coal owners reduced wages and extended working hours after the government subsidy ended"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-HIST-BRIT2-2","scaffold":"Geddes Axe: Public spending cuts recommended in the early 1920s to reduce the budget deficit. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"A student applies Geddes Axe to a case. Which judgement is strongest?","options":{"A":"Britain was affected through exports and industry even though the financial crash was not itself a British event","B":"It made the return to peacetime difficult and strengthened demands for state protection","C":"It exposed class tension but ended after limited wider support and state preparation","D":"It protected financial confidence but reduced demand and worsened the social impact of unemployment"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-2m-3"},{"id":"HIST-BRIT2-03","spec":"AQA-2M-BRIT2","stem":"What was the outcome of the General Strike for the miners it was called to support?","options":{"A":"They won a restoration of the subsidy and kept their existing hours and pay","B":"They were left out alone until November, then returned on worse terms","C":"They accepted the Samuel Commission's terms and returned to work with the TUC","D":"They secured shorter hours in exchange for accepting a reduction in their wages"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-HIST-GENSTRIKE","scaffold":"The TUC called the strike off on 12 May 1926, after nine days, having obtained no concessions. The miners remained out until November and returned on longer hours and lower pay — exactly what the lockout had been about. The consequence ran against the unions: the Trade Disputes Act 1927 made sympathetic strikes illegal and switched union political funding to contracting-in, cutting Labour's income.","reforge":{"stem":"Which evidence most strongly suggests the TUC did not intend the strike as a bid for political power?","options":{"A":"The strike involved only a minority of the total trade union membership","B":"The government had prepared for a strike for nine months in advance","C":"Baldwin's broadcasts described the strike as a challenge to the constitution","D":"The General Council called it off unilaterally after nine days without terms"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-2m-3"},{"id":"HIST-BRIT2-04","spec":"AQA-2M-BRIT2","stem":"How should miners' lockout be defined in this course?","options":{"A":"An assessment of household resources used to determine eligibility for unemployment assistance","B":"The commission that investigated the coal industry before the General Strike","C":"A nationwide stoppage called in support of locked-out coal miners","D":"Coal owners reduced wages and extended working hours after the government subsidy ended"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-HIST-GENSTRIKE","scaffold":"miners' lockout: Coal owners reduced wages and extended working hours after the government subsidy ended. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"How should miners' lockout be evaluated?","options":{"A":"It exposed class tension but ended after limited wider support and state preparation","B":"It reflected the coal industry's structural weakness and provoked the 1926 crisis","C":"Its recommendations were rejected by miners because they implied wage cuts and longer hours","D":"It demonstrated Labour's growing legitimacy but had limited room for radical policy"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-2m-3"},{"id":"HIST-BRIT2-05","spec":"AQA-2M-BRIT2","stem":"Which example best illustrates Samuel Commission?","options":{"A":"The commission that investigated the coal industry before the General Strike","B":"Labour's first government, led by Ramsay MacDonald, without a Commons majority","C":"Coal owners reduced wages and extended working hours after the government subsidy ended","D":"A Labour administration formed after the 1929 election without an overall majority"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-HIST-GENSTRIKE","scaffold":"Samuel Commission: The commission that investigated the coal industry before the General Strike. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"What is the defensible view of Samuel Commission?","options":{"A":"It demonstrated Labour's growing legitimacy but had limited room for radical policy","B":"It reflected the coal industry's structural weakness and provoked the 1926 crisis","C":"Its recommendations were rejected by miners because they implied wage cuts and longer hours","D":"Britain was affected through exports and industry even though the financial crash was not itself a British event"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-2m-3"},{"id":"HIST-BRIT2-06","spec":"AQA-2M-BRIT2","stem":"What role does minority Labour government 1924 play in the topic?","options":{"A":"A Labour administration formed after the 1929 election without an overall majority","B":"Labour's first government, led by Ramsay MacDonald, without a Commons majority","C":"The commission that investigated the coal industry before the General Strike","D":"A period of falling output and severe regional unemployment during the early 1930s"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-HIST-BRIT2-6","scaffold":"minority Labour government 1924: Labour's first government, led by Ramsay MacDonald, without a Commons majority. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"In a comparison question, what should a student say about minority Labour government 1924?","options":{"A":"Britain was affected through exports and industry even though the financial crash was not itself a British event","B":"Its recommendations were rejected by miners because they implied wage cuts and longer hours","C":"It faced the Depression and internal disagreement over how to respond to unemployment","D":"It demonstrated Labour's growing legitimacy but had limited room for radical policy"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-2m-3"},{"id":"HIST-BRIT2-07","spec":"AQA-2M-BRIT2","stem":"Why is second Labour government 1929 significant?","options":{"A":"Labour's first government, led by Ramsay MacDonald, without a Commons majority","B":"The international financial crisis reduced trade, investment and demand","C":"A Labour administration formed after the 1929 election without an overall majority","D":"A period of falling output and severe regional unemployment during the early 1930s"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-HIST-BRIT2-7","scaffold":"second Labour government 1929: A Labour administration formed after the 1929 election without an overall majority. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"Which outcome is most closely connected with second Labour government 1929?","options":{"A":"It faced the Depression and internal disagreement over how to respond to unemployment","B":"It demonstrated Labour's growing legitimacy but had limited room for radical policy","C":"Britain was affected through exports and industry even though the financial crash was not itself a British event","D":"It reduced public spending but caused resentment because families felt their privacy and dignity were invaded"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-2m-3"},{"id":"HIST-BRIT2-08","spec":"AQA-2M-BRIT2","stem":"What is the supported by the evidence view of Wall Street Crash impact?","options":{"A":"An assessment of household resources used to determine eligibility for unemployment assistance","B":"A period of falling output and severe regional unemployment during the early 1930s","C":"A Labour administration formed after the 1929 election without an overall majority","D":"The international financial crisis reduced trade, investment and demand"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-HIST-BRIT2-8","scaffold":"Wall Street Crash impact: The international financial crisis reduced trade, investment and demand. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"A historian or political analyst considers Wall Street Crash impact. Which conclusion is sound?","options":{"A":"It faced the Depression and internal disagreement over how to respond to unemployment","B":"Britain was affected through exports and industry even though the financial crash was not itself a British event","C":"Its effects were uneven, with the staple industrial regions suffering most","D":"It suggested voters wanted a change of government without rejecting the main elements of the post-war settlement"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-2m-4"},{"id":"HIST-BRIT2-09","spec":"AQA-2M-BRIT2","stem":"Which option about living standards in Britain in the 1930s is best supported by the evidence?","options":{"A":"Living standards fell across the country as unemployment rose everywhere","B":"Living standards rose across the country as prices fell faster than wages","C":"Real wages rose for those in work while staple regions saw mass unemployment","D":"Living standards were broadly unchanged, with regional differences cancelling out"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-HIST-THIRTIES","scaffold":"Both halves are true at once. For those IN WORK real wages rose roughly 15% across the decade, because prices fell faster than wages, and private housebuilding boomed — nearly 293,000 houses in 1936. Meanwhile Jarrow and Merthyr recorded unemployment around 67% and 62% in 1934. The question 'were the 1930s the hungry thirties?' is answered by disaggregating by region, industry and employment status, not by picking a side.","reforge":{"stem":"Coventry's unemployment was around 5% in the mid-1930s while Jarrow's was around 67%. What best explains the gap?","options":{"A":"Coventry received government assistance under the Special Areas Act","B":"Jarrow's workforce was less skilled and harder for employers to retrain","C":"Coventry had a younger population that migrated more readily for work","D":"Coventry made cars and electrical goods; Jarrow depended on ships"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-2m-4"},{"id":"HIST-BRIT2-10","spec":"AQA-2M-BRIT2","stem":"Which distinction helps identify Hungry Thirties debate?","options":{"A":"An assessment of household resources used to determine eligibility for unemployment assistance","B":"Whether the 1930s meant hardship or rising living standards for many","C":"A period of falling output and severe regional unemployment during the early 1930s","D":"A government formed across party lines during the financial crisis and led by MacDonald"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-HIST-THIRTIES","scaffold":"Hungry Thirties debate: Whether the 1930s meant hardship or rising living standards for many. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"Which interpretation of Hungry Thirties debate is most accurate?","options":{"A":"It allowed interest rates to fall and supported recovery, although it signalled financial weakness","B":"Its effects were uneven, with the staple industrial regions suffering most","C":"It reduced public spending but caused resentment because families felt their privacy and dignity were invaded","D":"Regional and class differences mean a single national description is misleading"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-2m-4"},{"id":"HIST-BRIT2-11","spec":"AQA-2M-BRIT2","stem":"A teacher asks for an accurate account of means test. Which answer works?","options":{"A":"Whether the 1930s meant hardship or rising living standards for many","B":"A government formed across party lines during the financial crisis and led by MacDonald","C":"An assessment of household resources used to determine eligibility for unemployment assistance","D":"Britain, France and Israel attacked Egypt after Nasser nationalised the canal, then withdrew under pressure"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-HIST-THIRTIES","scaffold":"means test: An assessment of household resources used to determine eligibility for unemployment assistance. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"What does a case involving means test show?","options":{"A":"It reduced public spending but caused resentment because families felt their privacy and dignity were invaded","B":"Regional and class differences mean a single national description is misleading","C":"It restored confidence but weakened Labour and enabled Conservative dominance","D":"It appeared to preserve peace temporarily but weakened Czechoslovakia and encouraged further German expansion"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-2m-4"},{"id":"HIST-BRIT2-12","spec":"AQA-2M-BRIT2","stem":"Who led the National Government formed in August 1931, and what did his own party do in response?","options":{"A":"Stanley Baldwin, whom the Conservatives re-elected as leader immediately afterwards","B":"Ramsay MacDonald, whom Labour endorsed as its leader in the national interest","C":"Philip Snowden, who resigned from Labour before agreeing to form the government","D":"Ramsay MacDonald, whom the Labour Party expelled for agreeing to lead it"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-HIST-NATGOV","scaffold":"MacDonald, the LABOUR Prime Minister, formed the National Government after his cabinet split over a proposed 10% cut in unemployment benefit. Labour expelled him, Snowden and Thomas. In substance the Conservatives dominated — after the October 1931 election the government held 554 seats, of which 473 were Conservative, and Labour fell from 288 seats to 52. So: a Conservative-dominated administration fronted by a Labour Prime Minister his party regarded as a traitor.","reforge":{"stem":"Why is it misleading to call the National Government simply 'a Conservative government'?","options":{"A":"Its policies followed Labour's 1929 manifesto more closely than the Conservatives'","B":"The Conservatives held fewer than half the government's seats after October 1931","C":"It was a genuine coalition in which the three parties held equal numbers of posts","D":"It was led by a Labour Prime Minister, though Conservatives supplied most MPs"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-2m-4"},{"id":"HIST-BRIT2-13","spec":"AQA-2M-BRIT2","stem":"What was the main economic consequence of Britain leaving the gold standard in September 1931?","options":{"A":"Sterling fell and policy was freed, allowing the cheap money that drove recovery","B":"Sterling rose sharply, cutting import costs and raising real incomes for households","C":"The pound became worthless abroad, forcing Britain to rely on imperial preference","D":"Interest rates had to be raised to defend the currency, deepening the depression"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-HIST-GOLD","scaffold":"Sterling fell about 25%, from $4.86 to roughly $3.40, making exports competitive. Freed from defending the parity, the Bank cut Bank Rate to 2% in 1932 and held it there — the cheap money that financed the housing boom and underpinned a recovery stronger than most of Europe's by 1937. Sidney Webb's reaction, that nobody had told them they could do that, captures how the constraint had been treated as a law of nature.","reforge":{"stem":"Why is the 1925 return to gold relevant to judging the 1931 departure?","options":{"A":"The 1925 decision was reversed within months, so 1931 merely confirmed it","B":"Both decisions were taken by Churchill, who admitted the second was an error","C":"The 1925 return overvalued sterling, forcing wage cuts behind the General Strike","D":"Britain had left gold in 1914, so the 1925 return was the genuine break with policy"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-2m-4"},{"id":"HIST-BRIT2-14","spec":"AQA-2M-BRIT2","stem":"Which feature is most closely associated with cheap money policy?","options":{"A":"The expansion of military spending during the later 1930s","B":"Low interest rates introduced after leaving the gold standard","C":"Britain abandoned the fixed gold parity of sterling in 1931","D":"Expanding sectors such as cars, electrical goods and chemicals in inter-war Britain"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-HIST-GOLD","scaffold":"cheap money policy: Low interest rates introduced after leaving the gold standard. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"A student applies cheap money policy to a case. Which judgement is strongest?","options":{"A":"They created jobs and consumer opportunities but were concentrated in the Midlands and South","B":"It allowed interest rates to fall and supported recovery, although it signalled financial weakness","C":"It reduced unemployment in some industries while reflecting the international crisis and delaying cuts","D":"It encouraged housebuilding and consumer spending in the more prosperous regions"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-2m-4"},{"id":"HIST-BRIT2-15","spec":"AQA-2M-BRIT2","stem":"Which consideration facing Chamberlain in September 1938 is most often left out of student answers?","options":{"A":"That Hitler had already broken the Munich terms before the agreement was signed","B":"That the Dominions had signalled they would not join a war over Czechoslovakia","C":"That the RAF already held a decisive advantage in fighter aircraft over the Luftwaffe","D":"That the Soviet Union had offered a firm military alliance which Britain refused"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-HIST-APPEASE","scaffold":"Judge on 1938 information. The Chiefs of Staff advised Britain could not fight Germany, Italy and Japan at once; radar and fighter production needed time; the Dominions would not follow; public memory of 1914–18 was overwhelming; and the USSR was distrusted after the purges, with no land route to Prague. Against that: Munich handed Germany the Skoda works and the Czech fortifications. What changed was March 1939, when the occupation of Prague destroyed appeasement's own premise.","reforge":{"stem":"Which event best marks the point at which appeasement's central assumption collapsed?","options":{"A":"The remilitarisation of the Rhineland in March 1936, unopposed","B":"The Anschluss with Austria in March 1938","C":"The occupation of the rest of Czechoslovakia in March 1939","D":"The Nazi-Soviet Pact of August 1939"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-2m-4"},{"id":"HIST-BRIT2-16","spec":"AQA-2M-BRIT2","stem":"How should new industries be defined in this course?","options":{"A":"The 1942 plan to tackle the five giants of Want, Disease, Ignorance, Squalor and Idleness","B":"The contrast between prosperous areas and depressed coal, shipbuilding and steel regions","C":"The expansion of military spending during the later 1930s","D":"Expanding sectors such as cars, electrical goods and chemicals in inter-war Britain"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-HIST-THIRTIES","scaffold":"new industries: Expanding sectors such as cars, electrical goods and chemicals in inter-war Britain. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"How should new industries be evaluated?","options":{"A":"It reduced unemployment in some industries while reflecting the international crisis and delaying cuts","B":"They created jobs and consumer opportunities but were concentrated in the Midlands and South","C":"It explains why national averages could conceal serious local poverty","D":"It showed that recovery and fear of extremism could outweigh dissatisfaction with unemployment"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-2m-4"},{"id":"HIST-BRIT2-17","spec":"AQA-2M-BRIT2","stem":"Which example best illustrates regional inequality?","options":{"A":"The contrast between prosperous areas and depressed coal, shipbuilding and steel regions","B":"Publicly built homes provided by local authorities","C":"Expanding sectors such as cars, electrical goods and chemicals in inter-war Britain","D":"The 1942 plan to tackle the five giants of Want, Disease, Ignorance, Squalor and Idleness"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-HIST-THIRTIES","scaffold":"regional inequality: The contrast between prosperous areas and depressed coal, shipbuilding and steel regions. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"What is the defensible view of regional inequality?","options":{"A":"It improved living conditions and supported suburban development, though access was uneven","B":"They created jobs and consumer opportunities but were concentrated in the Midlands and South","C":"It explains why national averages could conceal serious local poverty","D":"It showed that recovery and fear of extremism could outweigh dissatisfaction with unemployment"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-2m-4"},{"id":"HIST-BRIT2-18","spec":"AQA-2M-BRIT2","stem":"What role does council housing play in the topic?","options":{"A":"An election that consolidated the National Government's position","B":"Publicly built homes provided by local authorities","C":"The contrast between prosperous areas and depressed coal, shipbuilding and steel regions","D":"The policy of making concessions to avoid war with aggressive dictatorships"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-HIST-BRIT2-18","scaffold":"council housing: Publicly built homes provided by local authorities. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"In a comparison question, what should a student say about council housing?","options":{"A":"It was supported by war-weariness and strategic weakness but ultimately failed to contain Hitler","B":"It explains why national averages could conceal serious local poverty","C":"It showed that recovery and fear of extremism could outweigh dissatisfaction with unemployment","D":"It improved living conditions and supported suburban development, though access was uneven"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-2m-4"},{"id":"HIST-BRIT2-19","spec":"AQA-2M-BRIT2","stem":"Why is 1935 election significant?","options":{"A":"Publicly built homes provided by local authorities","B":"The policy of making concessions to avoid war with aggressive dictatorships","C":"An election that consolidated the National Government's position","D":"The 1938 agreement that transferred the Sudetenland from Czechoslovakia to Germany"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-HIST-NATGOV","scaffold":"1935 election: An election that consolidated the National Government's position. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"Which outcome is most closely connected with 1935 election?","options":{"A":"It showed that recovery and fear of extremism could outweigh dissatisfaction with unemployment","B":"It improved living conditions and supported suburban development, though access was uneven","C":"It was supported by war-weariness and strategic weakness but ultimately failed to contain Hitler","D":"It appeared to preserve peace temporarily but weakened Czechoslovakia and encouraged further German expansion"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-2m-4"},{"id":"HIST-BRIT2-20","spec":"AQA-2M-BRIT2","stem":"What is the supported by the evidence view of appeasement?","options":{"A":"The 1942 plan to tackle the five giants of Want, Disease, Ignorance, Squalor and Idleness","B":"The 1938 agreement that transferred the Sudetenland from Czechoslovakia to Germany","C":"An election that consolidated the National Government's position","D":"The policy of making concessions to avoid war with aggressive dictatorships"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-HIST-APPEASE","scaffold":"appeasement: The policy of making concessions to avoid war with aggressive dictatorships. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"A historian or political analyst considers appeasement. Which conclusion is sound?","options":{"A":"It showed that recovery and fear of extremism could outweigh dissatisfaction with unemployment","B":"It was supported by war-weariness and strategic weakness but ultimately failed to contain Hitler","C":"It appeared to preserve peace temporarily but weakened Czechoslovakia and encouraged further German expansion","D":"It coordinated national mobilisation and made post-war reconstruction a central political question"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-2m-4"},{"id":"HIST-BRIT2-21","spec":"AQA-2M-BRIT2","stem":"How does Munich Agreement affect the system or period being studied?","options":{"A":"The 1938 agreement that transferred the Sudetenland from Czechoslovakia to Germany","B":"The idea that wartime society mobilised across class and regional lines","C":"The policy of making concessions to avoid war with aggressive dictatorships","D":"The 1942 plan to tackle the five giants of Want, Disease, Ignorance, Squalor and Idleness"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-HIST-APPEASE","scaffold":"Munich Agreement: The 1938 agreement that transferred the Sudetenland from Czechoslovakia to Germany. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"Why might Munich Agreement matter in this context?","options":{"A":"It captures shared sacrifice but can obscure inequalities in experience and reward","B":"It was supported by war-weariness and strategic weakness but ultimately failed to contain Hitler","C":"It appeared to preserve peace temporarily but weakened Czechoslovakia and encouraged further German expansion","D":"It suggested voters wanted a change of government without rejecting the main elements of the post-war settlement"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-2m-4"},{"id":"HIST-BRIT2-22","spec":"AQA-2M-BRIT2","stem":"Which distinction helps identify People's War?","options":{"A":"The movement of children from vulnerable urban areas to safer districts","B":"The idea that wartime society mobilised across class and regional lines","C":"The 1938 agreement that transferred the Sudetenland from Czechoslovakia to Germany","D":"The 1942 plan to tackle the five giants of Want, Disease, Ignorance, Squalor and Idleness"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-HIST-BRIT2-22","scaffold":"People's War: The idea that wartime society mobilised across class and regional lines. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"Which interpretation of People's War is most accurate?","options":{"A":"It aimed to make supply fairer but required bureaucracy and encouraged black markets","B":"It appeared to preserve peace temporarily but weakened Czechoslovakia and encouraged further German expansion","C":"It exposed class and regional differences while creating new contact between communities","D":"It captures shared sacrifice but can obscure inequalities in experience and reward"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-2m-5"},{"id":"HIST-BRIT2-23","spec":"AQA-2M-BRIT2","stem":"A teacher asks for an accurate account of wartime evacuation. Which answer works?","options":{"A":"The idea that wartime society mobilised across class and regional lines","B":"The controlled distribution of scarce food and consumer goods","C":"The movement of children from vulnerable urban areas to safer districts","D":"The 1942 plan to tackle the five giants of Want, Disease, Ignorance, Squalor and Idleness"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-HIST-BRIT2-23","scaffold":"wartime evacuation: The movement of children from vulnerable urban areas to safer districts. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"What does a case involving wartime evacuation show?","options":{"A":"It exposed class and regional differences while creating new contact between communities","B":"It captures shared sacrifice but can obscure inequalities in experience and reward","C":"It aimed to make supply fairer but required bureaucracy and encouraged black markets","D":"It coordinated national mobilisation and made post-war reconstruction a central political question"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-2m-5"},{"id":"HIST-BRIT2-24","spec":"AQA-2M-BRIT2","stem":"Which consequence can be linked to rationing?","options":{"A":"The cross-party government led by Churchill during the Second World War","B":"A return to wartime rationing across every product","C":"The movement of children from vulnerable urban areas to safer districts","D":"The controlled distribution of scarce food and consumer goods"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-HIST-BRIT2-24","scaffold":"rationing: The controlled distribution of scarce food and consumer goods. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"Which piece of evidence would best support an argument about rationing?","options":{"A":"It exposed class and regional differences while creating new contact between communities","B":"It aimed to make supply fairer but required bureaucracy and encouraged black markets","C":"It helped shape expectations of comprehensive post-war social security","D":"It coordinated national mobilisation and made post-war reconstruction a central political question"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-2m-5"},{"id":"HIST-BRIT2-25","spec":"AQA-2M-BRIT2","stem":"What did the Beveridge Report of 1942 itself put into law?","options":{"A":"National Insurance and the National Health Service, both established by the Report","B":"A universal pension and free secondary education, implemented within two years","C":"Nothing — it was a report, and the legislation followed between 1944 and 1948","D":"Family allowances only, with the remaining four giants addressed after 1945"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-HIST-BEVERIDGE","scaffold":"The Report identified five giants — Want, Disease, Ignorance, Squalor, Idleness — sold over 600,000 copies and shaped the political argument, but legislated nothing. Want: National Insurance Act 1946 and National Assistance Act 1948. Disease: NHS Act 1946, operational July 1948. Ignorance: Butler Education Act 1944 — a Conservative minister in Churchill's coalition, and the earliest of the five. Squalor: New Towns Act 1946. Idleness: the 1944 Employment White Paper.","reforge":{"stem":"Which fact most complicates the claim that the welfare state was a Labour creation?","options":{"A":"Churchill's coalition passed the Butler Education Act in 1944","B":"The 1946 National Insurance Act retained the contributory principle of 1911","C":"Beveridge was a Liberal, not a member of the Labour Party","D":"The Conservatives kept the NHS intact when they returned to office in 1951"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-2m-5"},{"id":"HIST-BRIT2-26","spec":"AQA-2M-BRIT2","stem":"Which feature is most closely associated with wartime coalition?","options":{"A":"Labour won a decisive majority after the Second World War","B":"The cross-party government led by Churchill during the Second World War","C":"A Soviet ultimatum threatening direct military intervention against British forces","D":"The transfer of major industries such as coal, rail and steel into public ownership"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-HIST-BRIT2-26","scaffold":"wartime coalition: The cross-party government led by Churchill during the Second World War. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"A student applies wartime coalition to a case. Which judgement is strongest?","options":{"A":"It aimed to modernise strategic industries but produced mixed results and did not remove all problems","B":"It helped shape expectations of comprehensive post-war social security","C":"Voters sought social reform and judged the Conservatives' record and campaign insufficient","D":"It coordinated national mobilisation and made post-war reconstruction a central political question"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-2m-5"},{"id":"HIST-BRIT2-27","spec":"AQA-2M-BRIT2","stem":"Which evidence most strongly undermines the claim that Labour's 1945 victory was caused by Churchill's election campaign?","options":{"A":"Churchill remained personally more popular than Attlee throughout the campaign","B":"Labour's manifesto was published later than the Conservative one that year","C":"The forces' vote was counted separately and broke heavily towards Labour","D":"Gallup showed Labour ahead from 1942 and by-elections were already swinging"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-HIST-1945","scaffold":"The timing rules the campaign out as the cause: Gallup put Labour ahead from 1942 and by-elections swung against the government well before June 1945, so a broadcast cannot explain a movement three years old. The stronger explanations are memory of the 1930s — the Conservatives as the party of Jarrow and Munich — and Labour's ownership of Beveridge, dramatised when 97 Labour backbenchers defied their whip in February 1943 to demand immediate implementation.","reforge":{"stem":"What does the February 1943 Commons vote on the Beveridge Report show about the parties?","options":{"A":"That the coalition was united behind immediate implementation of the Report","B":"That Labour backbenchers pressed for implementation while ministers temporised","C":"That the Conservatives had already committed to legislating the Report in full","D":"That Beveridge's proposals commanded no majority in the Commons at that date"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-2m-5"},{"id":"HIST-BRIT2-28","spec":"AQA-2M-BRIT2","stem":"How should Clement Attlee be defined in this course?","options":{"A":"The transfer of major industries such as coal, rail and steel into public ownership","B":"The 1948 establishment of a health service free at the point of use","C":"Labour won a decisive majority after the Second World War","D":"Prime Minister of the Labour government from 1945 to 1951"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-HIST-1945","scaffold":"Clement Attlee: Prime Minister of the Labour government from 1945 to 1951. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"How should Clement Attlee be evaluated?","options":{"A":"Voters sought social reform and judged the Conservatives' record and campaign insufficient","B":"His government oversaw nationalisation, the NHS and the expansion of social security","C":"It represented a major expansion of collective welfare and state responsibility","D":"It aimed to modernise strategic industries but produced mixed results and did not remove all problems"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-2m-5"},{"id":"HIST-BRIT2-29","spec":"AQA-2M-BRIT2","stem":"Which example best illustrates creation of the NHS?","options":{"A":"The 1948 establishment of a health service free at the point of use","B":"The transfer of major industries such as coal, rail and steel into public ownership","C":"Prime Minister of the Labour government from 1945 to 1951","D":"A system of public provision designed to protect citizens from social risks"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-HIST-BEVERIDGE","scaffold":"creation of the NHS: The 1948 establishment of a health service free at the point of use. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"What is the defensible view of creation of the NHS?","options":{"A":"It aimed to modernise strategic industries but produced mixed results and did not remove all problems","B":"His government oversaw nationalisation, the NHS and the expansion of social security","C":"It represented a major expansion of collective welfare and state responsibility","D":"Post-war welfare expanded security but remained dependent on economic resources and political choices"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-2m-5"},{"id":"HIST-BRIT2-30","spec":"AQA-2M-BRIT2","stem":"Which policy area shows that the post-war consensus did NOT mean the parties had stopped disagreeing?","options":{"A":"Iron and steel, denationalised in 1953 and renationalised in 1967","B":"The National Health Service, which both parties maintained after 1948","C":"The commitment to full employment, accepted by both front benches","D":"The independent nuclear deterrent, continued by successive governments"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-HIST-CONSENSUS","scaffold":"Consensus means a shared FRAMEWORK — mixed economy, welfare state, full employment, NATO — inside which the parties fought hard about ownership, priorities and scale. Iron and steel changed hands twice; road haulage was largely denationalised; and Macmillan deliberately outbuilt Labour on council housing with his 300,000 target. Note the term is retrospective, popularised by Addison in 1975, and historians such as Pimlott have disputed that it existed at all.","reforge":{"stem":"Macmillan promised and delivered 300,000 houses a year. Why is this NOT good evidence of consensus?","options":{"A":"It was competition to outbuild Labour, not agreement on provision levels","B":"The houses were built by private developers rather than local authorities","C":"Labour had never committed to a specific housebuilding target of its own","D":"The target was announced but was not in fact met in any single year"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-2m-5"},{"id":"HIST-BRIT2-31","spec":"AQA-2M-BRIT2","stem":"Why is welfare state significant?","options":{"A":"The transfer of major industries such as coal, rail and steel into public ownership","B":"Broad agreement between major parties on welfare, mixed economy and demand management","C":"A system of public provision designed to protect citizens from social risks","D":"US economic assistance provided to European countries after the Second World War"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-HIST-BEVERIDGE","scaffold":"welfare state: A system of public provision designed to protect citizens from social risks. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"Which outcome is most closely connected with welfare state?","options":{"A":"Post-war welfare expanded security but remained dependent on economic resources and political choices","B":"It aimed to modernise strategic industries but produced mixed results and did not remove all problems","C":"It provided stability but did not mean that parties or voters agreed on every policy","D":"It suggested voters wanted a change of government without rejecting the main elements of the post-war settlement"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-2m-5"},{"id":"HIST-BRIT2-32","spec":"AQA-2M-BRIT2","stem":"What is the supported by the evidence view of post-war consensus?","options":{"A":"Britain, France and Israel attacked Egypt after Nasser nationalised the canal, then withdrew under pressure","B":"Continued controls and shortages caused by debt and rationing","C":"A system of public provision designed to protect citizens from social risks","D":"Broad agreement between major parties on welfare, mixed economy and demand management"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-HIST-CONSENSUS","scaffold":"post-war consensus: Broad agreement between major parties on welfare, mixed economy and demand management. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"A historian or political analyst considers post-war consensus. Which conclusion is sound?","options":{"A":"Post-war welfare expanded security but remained dependent on economic resources and political choices","B":"It provided stability but did not mean that parties or voters agreed on every policy","C":"Victory in war did not produce immediate prosperity and limited Labour's room for manoeuvre","D":"It exposed Britain's dependence on the USA and marked a sharp decline in imperial confidence"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-2m-5"},{"id":"HIST-BRIT2-33","spec":"AQA-2M-BRIT2","stem":"How does austerity after 1945 affect the system or period being studied?","options":{"A":"Continued controls and shortages caused by debt and rationing","B":"US economic assistance provided to European countries after the Second World War","C":"Broad agreement between major parties on welfare, mixed economy and demand management","D":"The accelerated process of decolonisation after the Second World War"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-HIST-BRIT2-33","scaffold":"austerity after 1945: Continued controls and shortages caused by debt and rationing. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"Why might austerity after 1945 matter in this context?","options":{"A":"It supported recovery but came with economic and strategic expectations","B":"It provided stability but did not mean that parties or voters agreed on every policy","C":"Victory in war did not produce immediate prosperity and limited Labour's room for manoeuvre","D":"It exposed Britain's dependence on the USA and marked a sharp decline in imperial confidence"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-2m-5"},{"id":"HIST-BRIT2-34","spec":"AQA-2M-BRIT2","stem":"Which distinction helps identify Marshall Aid?","options":{"A":"The accelerated process of decolonisation after the Second World War","B":"US economic assistance provided to European countries after the Second World War","C":"Continued controls and shortages caused by debt and rationing","D":"Britain, France and Israel attacked Egypt after Nasser nationalised the canal, then withdrew under pressure"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-HIST-BRIT2-34","scaffold":"Marshall Aid: US economic assistance provided to European countries after the Second World War. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"Which interpretation of Marshall Aid is most accurate?","options":{"A":"It exposed Britain's dependence on the USA and marked a sharp decline in imperial confidence","B":"Victory in war did not produce immediate prosperity and limited Labour's room for manoeuvre","C":"It reduced Britain's global power and changed the meaning of national influence","D":"It supported recovery but came with economic and strategic expectations"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-2m-5"},{"id":"HIST-BRIT2-35","spec":"AQA-2M-BRIT2","stem":"A teacher asks for an accurate account of end of empire. Which answer works?","options":{"A":"US economic assistance provided to European countries after the Second World War","B":"Britain, France and Israel attacked Egypt after Nasser nationalised the canal, then withdrew under pressure","C":"The accelerated process of decolonisation after the Second World War","D":"The Conservatives returned to office under Churchill despite the continuation of much welfare provision"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-HIST-BRIT2-35","scaffold":"end of empire: The accelerated process of decolonisation after the Second World War. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"What does a case involving end of empire show?","options":{"A":"It reduced Britain's global power and changed the meaning of national influence","B":"It supported recovery but came with economic and strategic expectations","C":"It exposed Britain's dependence on the USA and marked a sharp decline in imperial confidence","D":"It suggested voters wanted a change of government without rejecting the main elements of the post-war settlement"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-2m-5"},{"id":"HIST-BRIT2-36","spec":"AQA-2M-BRIT2","stem":"What forced Britain to halt the Suez operation in November 1956?","options":{"A":"Egyptian forces defeated the landings and retook Port Said within days","B":"A run on sterling, with the US refusing support without a ceasefire","C":"A Soviet ultimatum threatening direct military intervention against British forces","D":"The Commonwealth voted to expel Britain unless the operation was abandoned"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-HIST-SUEZ","scaffold":"Militarily the operation was succeeding — the landings took Port Said and the canal zone was being secured. What stopped it was financial and diplomatic: reserves fell sharply through November, and Eisenhower, furious at being deceived and facing an election, refused to back an IMF loan or release oil unless Britain accepted a ceasefire. Eden resigned in January 1957. The lesson contemporaries drew was that Britain could not act militarily against American opposition.","reforge":{"stem":"Why is Suez cited as evidence of British decline if the operation was militarily successful?","options":{"A":"Because the canal was permanently lost to British and French ownership","B":"Because it demonstrated the limits of British power against American pressure","C":"Because the armed forces proved unable to sustain an operation at that distance","D":"Because it triggered the immediate independence of the remaining African colonies"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-2m-6"},{"id":"HIST-BRIT2-37","spec":"AQA-2M-BRIT2","stem":"What does 1951 Conservative return refer to?","options":{"A":"The Conservatives returned to office under Churchill despite the continuation of much welfare provision","B":"The act reorganising secondary education and raising the school-leaving age","C":"Britain, France and Israel attacked Egypt after Nasser nationalised the canal, then withdrew under pressure","D":"Rising wages, consumer goods and home ownership for many households after austerity"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-HIST-CONSENSUS","scaffold":"1951 Conservative return: The Conservatives returned to office under Churchill despite the continuation of much welfare provision. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"Which example demonstrates 1951 Conservative return?","options":{"A":"It expanded opportunity while creating a selective tripartite structure that reproduced some inequalities","B":"It exposed Britain's dependence on the USA and marked a sharp decline in imperial confidence","C":"It suggested voters wanted a change of government without rejecting the main elements of the post-war settlement","D":"Britain was affected through exports and industry even though the financial crash was not itself a British event"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-2m-6"},{"id":"HIST-BRIT2-38","spec":"AQA-2M-BRIT2","stem":"Which feature is most closely associated with Butler Education Act 1944?","options":{"A":"Rising wages, consumer goods and home ownership for many households after austerity","B":"The act reorganising secondary education and raising the school-leaving age","C":"The Conservatives returned to office under Churchill despite the continuation of much welfare provision","D":"The period combined depression, war and reconstruction with major changes in state and society"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-HIST-BEVERIDGE","scaffold":"Butler Education Act 1944: The act reorganising secondary education and raising the school-leaving age. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"A student applies Butler Education Act 1944 to a case. Which judgement is strongest?","options":{"A":"Britain was affected through exports and industry even though the financial crash was not itself a British event","B":"It suggested voters wanted a change of government without rejecting the main elements of the post-war settlement","C":"It improved material living standards but did not erase regional or class differences","D":"It expanded opportunity while creating a selective tripartite structure that reproduced some inequalities"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-2m-6"},{"id":"HIST-BRIT2-39","spec":"AQA-2M-BRIT2","stem":"A student is revising 1950s affluence. Which description is accurate?","options":{"A":"The act reorganising secondary education and raising the school-leaving age","B":"The period combined depression, war and reconstruction with major changes in state and society","C":"Rising wages, consumer goods and home ownership for many households after austerity","D":"Coal owners reduced wages and extended working hours after the government subsidy ended"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-HIST-BRIT2-39","scaffold":"1950s affluence: Rising wages, consumer goods and home ownership for many households after austerity. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"What is the main significance of 1950s affluence?","options":{"A":"It improved material living standards but did not erase regional or class differences","B":"It expanded opportunity while creating a selective tripartite structure that reproduced some inequalities","C":"Weighing living standards, welfare and Britain's world role","D":"It made the return to peacetime difficult and strengthened demands for state protection"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-2m-6"},{"id":"HIST-BRIT2-40","spec":"AQA-2M-BRIT2","stem":"How should Britain 1918–1957 judgement be defined in this course?","options":{"A":"An assessment of household resources used to determine eligibility for unemployment assistance","B":"High unemployment after demobilisation and the contraction of wartime industries","C":"Rising wages, consumer goods and home ownership for many households after austerity","D":"The period combined depression, war and reconstruction with major changes in state and society"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-HIST-JUDGEMENT","scaffold":"Britain 1918–1957 judgement: The period combined depression, war and reconstruction with major changes in state and society. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"How should Britain 1918–1957 judgement be evaluated?","options":{"A":"It improved material living standards but did not erase regional or class differences","B":"Weighing living standards, welfare and Britain's world role","C":"It made the return to peacetime difficult and strengthened demands for state protection","D":"It protected financial confidence but reduced demand and worsened the social impact of unemployment"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-2m-6"},{"id":"HIST-2M6-GAPS-01","stem":"Why did Conservative governments after 1951 claim that Britain had achieved an 'affluent society'?","options":{"A":"Rising wages and wider ownership of consumer goods","B":"All poverty and regional inequality had disappeared","C":"Britain had abandoned a mixed economy completely","D":"The welfare state had been dismantled"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-HIST-2M6-GAPS-01","scaffold":"Post-war affluence was real but uneven, with poverty and inequality persisting in some groups and regions.","reforge":{"stem":"A student applies the idea to this case: Why did Conservative governments after 1951 claim that Britain had achieved an 'affluent society'?","options":{"A":"Rising wages and wider ownership of consumer goods","B":"Prime Minister of the Labour government from 1945 to 1951","C":"Britain abandoned the fixed gold parity of sterling in 1931","D":"The controlled distribution of scarce food and consumer goods"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-2m-6"},{"id":"HIST-2M6-GAPS-02","stem":"What was an important feature of the post-war consensus in Britain?","options":{"A":"Both parties rejected public ownership and national insurance","B":"Both parties accepted the mixed economy and welfare state","C":"The Conservatives and Labour shared one foreign policy on every issue","D":"Neither party supported government intervention in the economy"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-HIST-CONSENSUS","scaffold":"The consensus involved broad acceptance of welfare provision, full employment aims and a mixed economy, despite differences in emphasis.","reforge":{"stem":"In a practical example, which answer fits? What was an important feature of the post-war consensus in Britain?","options":{"A":"The commitment to full employment, accepted by both front benches","B":"Both parties accepted the mixed economy and welfare state","C":"The 1948 establishment of a health service free at the point of use","D":"Whether the 1930s meant hardship or rising living standards for many"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-2m-6"},{"id":"HIST-2M6-GAPS-03","stem":"Why was the 1956 Suez Crisis damaging to Anthony Eden's government?","options":{"A":"It led directly to the creation of the NHS","B":"It ended all Conservative support for decolonisation in 1951","C":"It exposed Britain's reduced international power","D":"It caused the General Strike of 1926"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-HIST-SUEZ","scaffold":"US opposition and the forced withdrawal demonstrated limits on Britain's ability to act as a great power.","reforge":{"stem":"A worked example tests the same principle: Why was the 1956 Suez Crisis damaging to Anthony Eden's government?","options":{"A":"The National Health Service, which both parties maintained after 1948","B":"The independent nuclear deterrent, continued by successive governments","C":"It exposed Britain's reduced international power","D":"Egyptian forces defeated the landings and retook Port Said within days"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-2m-6"},{"id":"HIST-2M6-GAPS-04","stem":"Which development helped change everyday life in Britain during the 1950s?","options":{"A":"The end of all private home ownership","B":"A return to wartime rationing across every product","C":"The removal of secondary education","D":"More cars, televisions and household appliances"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-HIST-2M6-GAPS-04","scaffold":"Consumer goods became more widely available, although access and prosperity remained uneven.","reforge":{"stem":"A new examination scenario asks: Which development helped change everyday life in Britain during the 1950s?","options":{"A":"The international financial crisis reduced trade, investment and demand","B":"The cross-party government led by Churchill during the Second World War","C":"Living standards fell across the country as unemployment rose everywhere","D":"More cars, televisions and household appliances"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-2m-6"},{"id":"HIST-2M6-GAPS-05","stem":"Why did the welfare state remain politically significant after 1951?","options":{"A":"It had become a widely expected part of social citizenship","B":"Most voters wanted all public services privatised immediately","C":"It applied only to wealthy households","D":"It was replaced by voluntary charity"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-HIST-CONSENSUS","scaffold":"The Conservatives maintained major welfare institutions because they had become embedded in expectations about government responsibility.","reforge":{"stem":"A student applies the idea to this case: Why did the welfare state remain politically significant after 1951?","options":{"A":"It had become a widely expected part of social citizenship","B":"The forces' vote was counted separately and broke heavily towards Labour","C":"Living standards rose across the country as prices fell faster than wages","D":"Labour's manifesto was published later than the Conservative one that year"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-2m-6"},{"id":"HIST-2M6-GAPS-06","stem":"What was a limitation of measuring post-war prosperity through average income alone?","options":{"A":"It always overstated poverty in every region","B":"It can hide regional and class differences","C":"It measured political participation rather than material conditions","D":"It excluded all changes in consumer spending"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-HIST-2M6-GAPS-06","scaffold":"Averages can hide unequal distribution, housing costs, insecurity and differences between regions.","reforge":{"stem":"In a practical example, which answer fits? What was a limitation of measuring post-war prosperity through average income alone?","options":{"A":"The Commonwealth voted to expel Britain unless the operation was abandoned","B":"It can hide regional and class differences","C":"They won a restoration of the subsidy and kept their existing hours and pay","D":"Family allowances only, with the remaining four giants addressed after 1945"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-2m-6"},{"id":"HIST-2M6-GAPS-07","stem":"How did decolonisation affect Britain's position in the 1950s?","options":{"A":"It expanded British control over every major colony","B":"It made Britain independent of the United States","C":"It shrank the empire and shifted Britain's focus","D":"It ended all migration to Britain"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-HIST-2M6-GAPS-07","scaffold":"Decolonisation reduced formal imperial power while Britain sought influence through diplomacy, trade and alliances.","reforge":{"stem":"A worked example tests the same principle: How did decolonisation affect Britain's position in the 1950s?","options":{"A":"They accepted the Samuel Commission's terms and returned to work with the TUC","B":"Ramsay MacDonald, whom Labour endorsed as its leader in the national interest","C":"It shrank the empire and shifted Britain's focus","D":"A universal pension and free secondary education, implemented within two years"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-2m-6"},{"id":"HIST-2M6-GAPS-08","stem":"Why did Macmillan's government emphasise that people had 'never had it so good'?","options":{"A":"It acknowledged that unemployment had reached zero everywhere","B":"It announced the end of the welfare state","C":"It described Britain's defeat at Suez","D":"It presented rising prosperity as Conservative success"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-HIST-2M6-GAPS-08","scaffold":"The phrase was political messaging that highlighted prosperity while critics pointed to inequality and persistent social problems.","reforge":{"stem":"A new examination scenario asks: Why did Macmillan's government emphasise that people had 'never had it so good'?","options":{"A":"Churchill remained personally more popular than Attlee throughout the campaign","B":"They secured shorter hours in exchange for accepting a reduction in their wages","C":"Philip Snowden, who resigned from Labour before agreeing to form the government","D":"It presented rising prosperity as Conservative success"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-2m-6"}]},"HIST-USA1":{"label":"USA 1865–1920","color":"#92400e","questions":[{"id":"HIST-USA1-01","spec":"AQA-1K-USA1","stem":"What was the central conflict that shaped Reconstruction between 1865 and 1877?","options":{"A":"A conflict between the Freedmen's Bureau and the Southern state legislatures over land","B":"A conflict between the Supreme Court and Congress over the wording of the amendments","C":"A conflict between President Johnson and Congress over the terms imposed on the South","D":"A conflict between Northern and Southern Republicans over the pace of railroad building"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-HIST-RECON","scaffold":"Presidential Reconstruction (1865–67) under Johnson was lenient: loyalty oaths, generous pardons, and Southern states responding with Black Codes. Congress, after the 1866 midterms returned a Republican supermajority, imposed Radical Reconstruction (1867–77): five military districts, ratification of the 14th Amendment, black male suffrage. Johnson was impeached in 1868 and survived by one vote. Merging the two loses the conflict that IS the period.","reforge":{"stem":"Which measure belongs to Radical rather than Presidential Reconstruction?","options":{"A":"Dividing the South into five military districts in 1867","B":"The issuing of generous pardons to former Confederates who swore loyalty","C":"The requirement that Southern states repudiate the Confederate war debt","D":"The readmission of states once they had ratified the Thirteenth Amendment"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-1k-1"},{"id":"HIST-USA1-02","spec":"AQA-1K-USA1","stem":"Which feature is most closely associated with Freedmen's Bureau?","options":{"A":"The constitutional amendment abolishing slavery","B":"A federal agency providing assistance and education to formerly enslaved people and poor Southerners","C":"The attempt to reunite the USA and define the status and rights of formerly enslaved people after the Civil War","D":"The USA became an industrial and international power while racial inequality and political corruption persisted"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-HIST-RECON","scaffold":"Freedmen's Bureau: A federal agency providing assistance and education to formerly enslaved people and poor Southerners. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"A student applies Freedmen's Bureau to a case. Which judgement is strongest?","options":{"A":"It gave the federal government a basis for challenging state discrimination","B":"It ended military conflict but left unresolved struggles over race, citizenship and federal power","C":"It ended legal slavery but did not by itself provide political equality or economic independence","D":"It created opportunities but was underfunded and faced resistance"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-1k-1"},{"id":"HIST-USA1-03","spec":"AQA-1K-USA1","stem":"Which pairing of amendment to content is correct?","options":{"A":"13th — equal protection; 14th — abolition of slavery; 15th — voting rights by race","B":"13th — voting rights by race; 14th — abolition of slavery; 15th — equal protection","C":"13th — abolition of slavery; 14th — voting rights by race; 15th — equal protection","D":"13th — abolition of slavery; 14th — equal protection; 15th — voting rights by race"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-HIST-AMENDMENTS","scaffold":"13th (1865) abolished slavery, with the clause 'except as a punishment for crime' that underwrote convict leasing. 14th (1868) established national citizenship, due process and equal protection, and crucially bound the STATES — which is what later made federal civil rights litigation possible. 15th (1870) barred denial of the vote on grounds of race, colour or previous servitude. Note what the 15th does NOT cover: sex, hence the 19th; and it left literacy tests and poll taxes untouched.","reforge":{"stem":"Why did women's suffrage require a separate amendment after the 15th?","options":{"A":"The 15th Amendment applied only to states that had seceded from the Union","B":"The 15th bars denial of the vote by race, and is silent on sex","C":"The 15th Amendment was struck down by the Supreme Court before it took effect","D":"The 15th Amendment expired after ten years and had to be replaced entirely"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-1k-1"},{"id":"HIST-USA1-04","spec":"AQA-1K-USA1","stem":"How should Fourteenth Amendment be defined in this course?","options":{"A":"Congressional policy using military districts and federal action to reshape Southern politics","B":"The amendment protecting male citizens' voting rights from racial denial","C":"The constitutional amendment abolishing slavery","D":"The amendment establishing national citizenship and equal protection under the law"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-HIST-AMENDMENTS","scaffold":"Fourteenth Amendment: The amendment establishing national citizenship and equal protection under the law. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"How should Fourteenth Amendment be evaluated?","options":{"A":"It ended legal slavery but did not by itself provide political equality or economic independence","B":"It gave the federal government a basis for challenging state discrimination","C":"It expanded formal political rights but was undermined by later restrictions and violence","D":"It allowed former Confederates to regain influence and produced conflict with Congress"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-1k-1"},{"id":"HIST-USA1-05","spec":"AQA-1K-USA1","stem":"Which example best illustrates Fifteenth Amendment?","options":{"A":"The amendment protecting male citizens' voting rights from racial denial","B":"Andrew Johnson's lenient approach to readmitting Southern states","C":"The amendment establishing national citizenship and equal protection under the law","D":"Congressional policy using military districts and federal action to reshape Southern politics"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-HIST-AMENDMENTS","scaffold":"Fifteenth Amendment: The amendment protecting male citizens' voting rights from racial denial. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"What is the defensible view of Fifteenth Amendment?","options":{"A":"It allowed former Confederates to regain influence and produced conflict with Congress","B":"It gave the federal government a basis for challenging state discrimination","C":"It expanded formal political rights but was undermined by later restrictions and violence","D":"The term reflected Southern resentment but was often used to discredit legitimate reformers"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-1k-1"},{"id":"HIST-USA1-06","spec":"AQA-1K-USA1","stem":"What role does Presidential Reconstruction play in the topic?","options":{"A":"Congressional policy using military districts and federal action to reshape Southern politics","B":"Andrew Johnson's lenient approach to readmitting Southern states","C":"The amendment protecting male citizens' voting rights from racial denial","D":"A hostile term for Northerners who moved to the South during Reconstruction"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-HIST-RECON","scaffold":"Presidential Reconstruction: Andrew Johnson's lenient approach to readmitting Southern states. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"In a comparison question, what should a student say about Presidential Reconstruction?","options":{"A":"The term reflected Southern resentment but was often used to discredit legitimate reformers","B":"It expanded formal political rights but was undermined by later restrictions and violence","C":"It expanded Black political participation but provoked determined white resistance","D":"It allowed former Confederates to regain influence and produced conflict with Congress"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-1k-1"},{"id":"HIST-USA1-07","spec":"AQA-1K-USA1","stem":"Why is Radical Reconstruction significant?","options":{"A":"Andrew Johnson's lenient approach to readmitting Southern states","B":"A hostile term for Northerners who moved to the South during Reconstruction","C":"Congressional policy using military districts and federal action to reshape Southern politics","D":"The 1896 Supreme Court decision upholding segregation under the doctrine of separate but equal"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-HIST-RECON","scaffold":"Radical Reconstruction: Congressional policy using military districts and federal action to reshape Southern politics. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"Which outcome is most closely connected with Radical Reconstruction?","options":{"A":"It expanded Black political participation but provoked determined white resistance","B":"It allowed former Confederates to regain influence and produced conflict with Congress","C":"The term reflected Southern resentment but was often used to discredit legitimate reformers","D":"It undermined the promise of equal citizenship and became embedded through law and custom"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-1k-1"},{"id":"HIST-USA1-08","spec":"AQA-1K-USA1","stem":"What is the supported by the evidence view of carpetbagger?","options":{"A":"The 1896 Supreme Court decision upholding segregation under the doctrine of separate but equal","B":"White Southern Democrats who sought to restore conservative control after Reconstruction","C":"Congressional policy using military districts and federal action to reshape Southern politics","D":"A hostile term for Northerners who moved to the South during Reconstruction"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-HIST-RECON","scaffold":"carpetbagger: A hostile term for Northerners who moved to the South during Reconstruction. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"A historian or political analyst considers carpetbagger. Which conclusion is sound?","options":{"A":"It expanded Black political participation but provoked determined white resistance","B":"The term reflected Southern resentment but was often used to discredit legitimate reformers","C":"Their success reduced federal protection and restored racial hierarchy","D":"It integrated markets and stimulated settlement but created powerful corporations and abuses"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-1k-1"},{"id":"HIST-USA1-09","spec":"AQA-1K-USA1","stem":"Registered black voters in Louisiana fell from 130,344 in 1896 to 1,342 in 1904. How was this achieved?","options":{"A":"Through poll taxes, literacy tests and grandfather clauses upheld by the courts","B":"Through the repeal of the Fifteenth Amendment by a two-thirds vote of Congress","C":"Through a Supreme Court ruling that the Fifteenth Amendment applied to men only","D":"Through the mass migration of black voters out of Louisiana to northern cities"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-HIST-RIGHTSDECLINE","scaffold":"No amendment was repealed. From the 1890 Mississippi Plan onwards, states wrote constitutions using poll taxes, literacy tests and 'understanding clauses' administered by white registrars, with grandfather clauses exempting whites. Williams v Mississippi (1898) upheld these as race-neutral on their face. That is a 99% collapse achieved without changing the Constitution — which is why the 1965 Voting Rights Act attacks the MACHINERY rather than restating a principle already 95 years old.","reforge":{"stem":"Why were disfranchising devices written to be formally colour-blind?","options":{"A":"To satisfy the Freedmen's Bureau, which reviewed all state election law","B":"To keep the support of poor white voters who also failed literacy tests","C":"So that they would survive challenge under the Fifteenth Amendment","D":"Because federal registrars supervised every Southern election after 1877"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-1k-1"},{"id":"HIST-USA1-10","spec":"AQA-1K-USA1","stem":"Which distinction helps identify sharecropping?","options":{"A":"A system of state and local segregation laws in the American South","B":"A farming system in which tenants paid rent through a share of the crop","C":"White Southern Democrats who sought to restore conservative control after Reconstruction","D":"The 1896 Supreme Court decision upholding segregation under the doctrine of separate but equal"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-HIST-RIGHTSDECLINE","scaffold":"sharecropping: A farming system in which tenants paid rent through a share of the crop. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"Which interpretation of sharecropping is most accurate?","options":{"A":"It created national prosperity while highlighting severe inequality and weak regulation","B":"Their success reduced federal protection and restored racial hierarchy","C":"It undermined the promise of equal citizenship and became embedded through law and custom","D":"It offered access to land but often trapped Black and poor white farmers in debt"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-1k-1"},{"id":"HIST-USA1-11","spec":"AQA-1K-USA1","stem":"Who made segregation law in the American South?","options":{"A":"Congress, through federal statutes passed in the decade after Reconstruction","B":"State legislatures and city governments, with federal courts permitting it","C":"The Supreme Court, which imposed segregation directly through Plessy v Ferguson","D":"Presidential executive orders issued in succession from Hayes through to Wilson"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-HIST-JIMCROW","scaffold":"Jim Crow was STATE and LOCAL law. The federal role was permissive: the Civil Rights Cases (1883) struck down the 1875 Civil Rights Act, Plessy (1896) upheld 'separate but equal', Williams (1898) upheld literacy tests. Washington also segregated its own civil service under Wilson from 1913. The distinction matters because it explains the remedy: since segregation was state law permitted by federal courts, undoing it required federal power turned the other way — Brown, the 1964 Act, the 1965 Act.","reforge":{"stem":"What did Plessy v Ferguson (1896) actually decide?","options":{"A":"That segregation was required in all public facilities across the Southern states","B":"That the Fourteenth Amendment did not apply to the states at all after 1877","C":"That racial classifications by state governments were unconstitutional in principle","D":"That separate facilities were lawful provided they were held to be equal"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-1k-1"},{"id":"HIST-USA1-12","spec":"AQA-1K-USA1","stem":"Which consequence can be linked to Plessy v Ferguson?","options":{"A":"A reform movement seeking to regulate corporations, improve democracy and address social problems","B":"A white supremacist organisation using intimidation and violence","C":"A system of state and local segregation laws in the American South","D":"The 1896 Supreme Court decision upholding segregation under the doctrine of separate but equal"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-HIST-JIMCROW","scaffold":"Plessy v Ferguson: The 1896 Supreme Court decision upholding segregation under the doctrine of separate but equal. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"Which piece of evidence would best support an argument about Plessy v Ferguson?","options":{"A":"It undermined the promise of equal citizenship and became embedded through law and custom","B":"It legitimised Jim Crow until later civil-rights challenges","C":"It sought to destroy Black political participation and enforce racial control","D":"It created national prosperity while highlighting severe inequality and weak regulation"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-1k-1"},{"id":"HIST-USA1-13","spec":"AQA-1K-USA1","stem":"What does Ku Klux Klan refer to?","options":{"A":"A white supremacist organisation using intimidation and violence","B":"The period of rapid industrial growth, wealth and political corruption after the Civil War","C":"The 1896 Supreme Court decision upholding segregation under the doctrine of separate but equal","D":"The rapid extension and consolidation of railway networks across the USA"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-HIST-JIMCROW","scaffold":"Ku Klux Klan: A white supremacist organisation using intimidation and violence. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"Which example demonstrates Ku Klux Klan?","options":{"A":"It created national prosperity while highlighting severe inequality and weak regulation","B":"It legitimised Jim Crow until later civil-rights challenges","C":"It sought to destroy Black political participation and enforce racial control","D":"It integrated markets and stimulated settlement but created powerful corporations and abuses"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-1k-1"},{"id":"HIST-USA1-14","spec":"AQA-1K-USA1","stem":"Which description of Gilded Age laissez-faire best fits the evidence?","options":{"A":"Government withdrew from the economy entirely, intervening in no market at all","B":"Government regulated business closely while refusing to subsidise any industry","C":"Government subsidised business while declining to regulate labour","D":"Government protected workers through federal law but left prices to the market"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-HIST-LAISSEZ","scaffold":"Government was highly active: land grants of some 130 million acres to the railroads, tariffs approaching 50%, federal troops and injunctions against strikers, and the Sherman Act used more readily against unions than trusts. What it declined to do was regulate wages, hours and conditions or protect workers' organisation. Laissez-faire here was SELECTIVE, not minimal — which is why an essay saying government 'did nothing' contradicts its own evidence a paragraph later.","reforge":{"stem":"Which fact is hardest to reconcile with a literal reading of Gilded Age laissez-faire?","options":{"A":"Federal land grants of some 130 million acres to the railroad companies","B":"The absence of any federal income tax before the Sixteenth Amendment","C":"The refusal of Congress to legislate on working hours in most industries","D":"The failure of states to establish workmen's compensation schemes early"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-1k-1"},{"id":"HIST-USA1-15","spec":"AQA-1K-USA1","stem":"A student is revising railroad expansion. Which description is accurate?","options":{"A":"The period of rapid industrial growth, wealth and political corruption after the Civil War","B":"A critical label for powerful industrialists accused of monopoly and exploitation","C":"The rapid extension and consolidation of railway networks across the USA","D":"The application of evolutionary language to justify competition and inequality in society"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-HIST-LAISSEZ","scaffold":"railroad expansion: The rapid extension and consolidation of railway networks across the USA. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"What is the main significance of railroad expansion?","options":{"A":"It integrated markets and stimulated settlement but created powerful corporations and abuses","B":"It created national prosperity while highlighting severe inequality and weak regulation","C":"It captures contemporary hostility but can oversimplify entrepreneurs' economic contributions","D":"It raised monetary and economic grievances but struggled to build a durable national majority"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-1k-1"},{"id":"HIST-USA1-16","spec":"AQA-1K-USA1","stem":"How should Robber Baron be defined in this course?","options":{"A":"The application of evolutionary language to justify competition and inequality in society","B":"A preference for limited government regulation of business","C":"The rapid extension and consolidation of railway networks across the USA","D":"A critical label for powerful industrialists accused of monopoly and exploitation"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-HIST-LAISSEZ","scaffold":"Robber Baron: A critical label for powerful industrialists accused of monopoly and exploitation. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"How should Robber Baron be evaluated?","options":{"A":"It integrated markets and stimulated settlement but created powerful corporations and abuses","B":"It captures contemporary hostility but can oversimplify entrepreneurs' economic contributions","C":"It encouraged enterprise but left workers and consumers exposed to corporate power","D":"It raised monetary and economic grievances but struggled to build a durable national majority"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-1k-1"},{"id":"HIST-USA1-17","spec":"AQA-1K-USA1","stem":"Which example best illustrates laissez-faire?","options":{"A":"A preference for limited government regulation of business","B":"Urban organisations exchanging services and jobs for electoral support","C":"A critical label for powerful industrialists accused of monopoly and exploitation","D":"The Democratic political machine that dominated New York City politics"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-HIST-LAISSEZ","scaffold":"laissez-faire: A preference for limited government regulation of business. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"What is the defensible view of laissez-faire?","options":{"A":"They helped immigrants access assistance but were associated with corruption and patronage","B":"It captures contemporary hostility but can oversimplify entrepreneurs' economic contributions","C":"It encouraged enterprise but left workers and consumers exposed to corporate power","D":"It illustrates how machine politics combined social support with electoral corruption"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-1k-1"},{"id":"HIST-USA1-18","spec":"AQA-1K-USA1","stem":"What role does political machines play in the topic?","options":{"A":"The Democratic political machine that dominated New York City politics","B":"Urban organisations exchanging services and jobs for electoral support","C":"A preference for limited government regulation of business","D":"The application of evolutionary language to justify competition and inequality in society"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-HIST-USA1-18","scaffold":"political machines: Urban organisations exchanging services and jobs for electoral support. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"In a comparison question, what should a student say about political machines?","options":{"A":"It raised monetary and economic grievances but struggled to build a durable national majority","B":"It encouraged enterprise but left workers and consumers exposed to corporate power","C":"It illustrates how machine politics combined social support with electoral corruption","D":"They helped immigrants access assistance but were associated with corruption and patronage"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-1k-1"},{"id":"HIST-USA1-19","spec":"AQA-1K-USA1","stem":"Why is Tammany Hall significant?","options":{"A":"Urban organisations exchanging services and jobs for electoral support","B":"Federal limits on entry introduced in response to nativist pressure","C":"The Democratic political machine that dominated New York City politics","D":"The application of evolutionary language to justify competition and inequality in society"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-HIST-USA1-19","scaffold":"Tammany Hall: The Democratic political machine that dominated New York City politics. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"Which outcome is most closely connected with Tammany Hall?","options":{"A":"It illustrates how machine politics combined social support with electoral corruption","B":"They helped immigrants access assistance but were associated with corruption and patronage","C":"It reflected racial and cultural anxieties rather than only labour-market concerns","D":"It raised monetary and economic grievances but struggled to build a durable national majority"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-1k-1"},{"id":"HIST-USA1-20","spec":"AQA-1K-USA1","stem":"What is the supported by the evidence view of immigration restriction?","options":{"A":"A movement representing farmers and workers against banks, railroads and established elites","B":"The application of evolutionary language to justify competition and inequality in society","C":"The Democratic political machine that dominated New York City politics","D":"Federal limits on entry introduced in response to nativist pressure"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-HIST-USA1-20","scaffold":"immigration restriction: Federal limits on entry introduced in response to nativist pressure. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"A historian or political analyst considers immigration restriction. Which conclusion is sound?","options":{"A":"It illustrates how machine politics combined social support with electoral corruption","B":"It reflected racial and cultural anxieties rather than only labour-market concerns","C":"It gave ideological support to limited government and racial hierarchy","D":"It raised monetary and economic grievances but struggled to build a durable national majority"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-1k-1"},{"id":"HIST-USA1-21","spec":"AQA-1K-USA1","stem":"How does Social Darwinism affect the system or period being studied?","options":{"A":"The application of evolutionary language to justify competition and inequality in society","B":"A movement representing farmers and workers against banks, railroads and established elites","C":"Federal limits on entry introduced in response to nativist pressure","D":"A reform movement seeking to regulate corporations, improve democracy and address social problems"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-HIST-LAISSEZ","scaffold":"Social Darwinism: The application of evolutionary language to justify competition and inequality in society. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"Why might Social Darwinism matter in this context?","options":{"A":"It raised monetary and economic grievances but struggled to build a durable national majority","B":"It reflected racial and cultural anxieties rather than only labour-market concerns","C":"It gave ideological support to limited government and racial hierarchy","D":"Supporters hoped it would ease farmers' debts, while opponents feared inflation"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-1k-1"},{"id":"HIST-USA1-22","spec":"AQA-1K-USA1","stem":"Which contrast between Populism and Progressivism is accurate?","options":{"A":"Populism was urban and middle-class; Progressivism was rural and farmer-led","B":"Both were rural movements, but Populism sought regulation and Progressivism silver","C":"Populism operated through both major parties; Progressivism formed its own party","D":"Populism was rural and farmer-led; Progressivism was urban and middle-class"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-HIST-POPPROG","scaffold":"Populism: 1890s, rural South and West, the indebted farmer, Free Silver and the subtreasury plan, working through the People's Party until Bryan's 1896 candidacy absorbed it into the Democrats. Progressivism: 1900s–1910s, urban, middle-class professionals and journalists, regulation and efficiency and direct democracy, working through BOTH major parties under Roosevelt and Wilson. Many Omaha Platform demands were later enacted by Progressives, but the base and the target differ.","reforge":{"stem":"Which demand belongs to the Populist rather than the Progressive programme?","options":{"A":"The direct primary, to take candidate selection away from party bosses","B":"Free Silver, to expand the money supply and relieve farm debt","C":"The Pure Food and Drug Act, to protect consumers from adulteration","D":"Prohibition of alcohol, pursued through state and then federal law"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-1k-2"},{"id":"HIST-USA1-23","spec":"AQA-1K-USA1","stem":"A teacher asks for an accurate account of Free Silver. Which answer works?","options":{"A":"A movement representing farmers and workers against banks, railroads and established elites","B":"A reform movement seeking to regulate corporations, improve democracy and address social problems","C":"The demand for unlimited coinage of silver to expand the money supply","D":"Journalists who exposed corruption, unsafe conditions and corporate abuses"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-HIST-POPPROG","scaffold":"Free Silver: The demand for unlimited coinage of silver to expand the money supply. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"What does a case involving Free Silver show?","options":{"A":"Supporters hoped it would ease farmers' debts, while opponents feared inflation","B":"13th — abolition of slavery; 14th — voting rights by race; 15th — equal protection","C":"It expanded government responsibility but did not consistently challenge racial segregation","D":"It aimed to restore competition but did not end corporate concentration completely"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-1k-2"},{"id":"HIST-USA1-24","spec":"AQA-1K-USA1","stem":"Which consequence can be linked to Progressivism?","options":{"A":"The USA became an industrial and international power while racial inequality and political corruption persisted","B":"Journalists who exposed corruption, unsafe conditions and corporate abuses","C":"The demand for unlimited coinage of silver to expand the money supply","D":"A reform movement seeking to regulate corporations, improve democracy and address social problems"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-HIST-POPPROG","scaffold":"Progressivism: A reform movement seeking to regulate corporations, improve democracy and address social problems. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"Which piece of evidence would best support an argument about Progressivism?","options":{"A":"Supporters hoped it would ease farmers' debts, while opponents feared inflation","B":"It expanded government responsibility but did not consistently challenge racial segregation","C":"Their investigations helped create public support for reform legislation","D":"It improved financial coordination and gave the federal government a stronger economic role"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-1k-2"},{"id":"HIST-USA1-25","spec":"AQA-1K-USA1","stem":"What does muckrakers refer to?","options":{"A":"Journalists who exposed corruption, unsafe conditions and corporate abuses","B":"Government action to break up or regulate monopolistic corporations","C":"A reform movement seeking to regulate corporations, improve democracy and address social problems","D":"Theodore Roosevelt's programme of fairness for consumers, labour and business"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-HIST-POPPROG","scaffold":"muckrakers: Journalists who exposed corruption, unsafe conditions and corporate abuses. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"Which example demonstrates muckrakers?","options":{"A":"It aimed to restore competition but did not end corporate concentration completely","B":"It expanded government responsibility but did not consistently challenge racial segregation","C":"Their investigations helped create public support for reform legislation","D":"It signalled a more active federal government and linked reform to presidential leadership"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-1k-2"},{"id":"HIST-USA1-26","spec":"AQA-1K-USA1","stem":"Which feature is most closely associated with trust-busting?","options":{"A":"Theodore Roosevelt's programme of fairness for consumers, labour and business","B":"Government action to break up or regulate monopolistic corporations","C":"Journalists who exposed corruption, unsafe conditions and corporate abuses","D":"Woodrow Wilson's reform programme targeting tariffs, banks and monopolies"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-HIST-POPPROG","scaffold":"trust-busting: Government action to break up or regulate monopolistic corporations. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"A student applies trust-busting to a case. Which judgement is strongest?","options":{"A":"It differed from Roosevelt's approach but also expanded federal economic regulation","B":"Their investigations helped create public support for reform legislation","C":"It signalled a more active federal government and linked reform to presidential leadership","D":"It aimed to restore competition but did not end corporate concentration completely"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-1k-2"},{"id":"HIST-USA1-27","spec":"AQA-1K-USA1","stem":"A student is revising Square Deal. Which description is accurate?","options":{"A":"Government action to break up or regulate monopolistic corporations","B":"Woodrow Wilson's reform programme targeting tariffs, banks and monopolies","C":"Theodore Roosevelt's programme of fairness for consumers, labour and business","D":"The campaign for women's voting rights culminating in the Nineteenth Amendment"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-HIST-POPPROG","scaffold":"Square Deal: Theodore Roosevelt's programme of fairness for consumers, labour and business. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"What is the main significance of Square Deal?","options":{"A":"It signalled a more active federal government and linked reform to presidential leadership","B":"It aimed to restore competition but did not end corporate concentration completely","C":"It differed from Roosevelt's approach but also expanded federal economic regulation","D":"It improved financial coordination and gave the federal government a stronger economic role"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-1k-2"},{"id":"HIST-USA1-28","spec":"AQA-1K-USA1","stem":"How should New Freedom be defined in this course?","options":{"A":"The campaign for women's voting rights culminating in the Nineteenth Amendment","B":"The law creating a central banking system for the USA","C":"Theodore Roosevelt's programme of fairness for consumers, labour and business","D":"Woodrow Wilson's reform programme targeting tariffs, banks and monopolies"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-HIST-POPPROG","scaffold":"New Freedom: Woodrow Wilson's reform programme targeting tariffs, banks and monopolies. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"How should New Freedom be evaluated?","options":{"A":"It signalled a more active federal government and linked reform to presidential leadership","B":"It differed from Roosevelt's approach but also expanded federal economic regulation","C":"It improved financial coordination and gave the federal government a stronger economic role","D":"It combined long-term organisation with changing arguments about citizenship and reform"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-1k-2"},{"id":"HIST-USA1-29","spec":"AQA-1K-USA1","stem":"Which example best illustrates Federal Reserve Act 1913?","options":{"A":"The law creating a central banking system for the USA","B":"The amendment authorising a federal income tax","C":"Woodrow Wilson's reform programme targeting tariffs, banks and monopolies","D":"The campaign for women's voting rights culminating in the Nineteenth Amendment"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-HIST-POPPROG","scaffold":"Federal Reserve Act 1913: The law creating a central banking system for the USA. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"What is the defensible view of Federal Reserve Act 1913?","options":{"A":"It provided revenue for government while making taxation more progressive","B":"It differed from Roosevelt's approach but also expanded federal economic regulation","C":"It improved financial coordination and gave the federal government a stronger economic role","D":"It ended military conflict but left unresolved struggles over race, citizenship and federal power"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-1k-2"},{"id":"HIST-USA1-30","spec":"AQA-1K-USA1","stem":"What role does Sixteenth Amendment play in the topic?","options":{"A":"The campaign for women's voting rights culminating in the Nineteenth Amendment","B":"The amendment authorising a federal income tax","C":"The law creating a central banking system for the USA","D":"The 1920 amendment preventing states from denying women the vote on sex grounds"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-HIST-POPPROG","scaffold":"Sixteenth Amendment: The amendment authorising a federal income tax. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"In a comparison question, what should a student say about Sixteenth Amendment?","options":{"A":"It achieved national voting rights but did not end racial or economic barriers","B":"It improved financial coordination and gave the federal government a stronger economic role","C":"It combined long-term organisation with changing arguments about citizenship and reform","D":"It provided revenue for government while making taxation more progressive"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-1k-2"},{"id":"HIST-USA1-31","spec":"AQA-1K-USA1","stem":"Why is women's suffrage movement significant?","options":{"A":"The amendment authorising a federal income tax","B":"The 1920 amendment preventing states from denying women the vote on sex grounds","C":"The campaign for women's voting rights culminating in the Nineteenth Amendment","D":"The movement of African Americans from the rural South to northern and western cities"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-HIST-USA1-31","scaffold":"women's suffrage movement: The campaign for women's voting rights culminating in the Nineteenth Amendment. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"Which outcome is most closely connected with women's suffrage movement?","options":{"A":"It combined long-term organisation with changing arguments about citizenship and reform","B":"It provided revenue for government while making taxation more progressive","C":"It achieved national voting rights but did not end racial or economic barriers","D":"It ended military conflict but left unresolved struggles over race, citizenship and federal power"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-1k-2"},{"id":"HIST-USA1-32","spec":"AQA-1K-USA1","stem":"What is the supported by the evidence view of Nineteenth Amendment?","options":{"A":"The claim that the USA could intervene in Latin America to prevent European involvement","B":"The movement of African Americans from the rural South to northern and western cities","C":"The campaign for women's voting rights culminating in the Nineteenth Amendment","D":"The 1920 amendment preventing states from denying women the vote on sex grounds"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-HIST-USA1-32","scaffold":"Nineteenth Amendment: The 1920 amendment preventing states from denying women the vote on sex grounds. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"A historian or political analyst considers Nineteenth Amendment. Which conclusion is sound?","options":{"A":"It combined long-term organisation with changing arguments about citizenship and reform","B":"It achieved national voting rights but did not end racial or economic barriers","C":"It reflected search for work and safety and transformed urban politics and culture","D":"It increased American global influence but also revived debate over isolationism"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-1k-2"},{"id":"HIST-USA1-33","spec":"AQA-1K-USA1","stem":"Why does the Great Migration matter to the later civil rights movement?","options":{"A":"It created black electorates in Northern cities politicians had to court","B":"It removed the black population from the South, ending the need for Jim Crow","C":"It was organised by the NAACP as a deliberate strategy to pressure Congress","D":"It followed the Montgomery bus boycott as a protest against Southern law"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-HIST-CIVILRIGHTS-START","scaffold":"From about 1916, millions moved from the rural South to Northern and Western cities. Because Northern states did not disfranchise, this created black voters concentrated in states with large electoral college votes — a constituency both parties had to weigh, which is a substantial part of why federal politicians began to move at all. It is also chronology: the migration long precedes Montgomery in 1955, as do the NAACP's founding in 1909, Executive Order 8802 in 1941 and Truman's desegregation of the forces in 1948.","reforge":{"stem":"What does the NAACP's litigation strategy before Brown reveal?","options":{"A":"That its legal victories were won by long-term planning","B":"That black plaintiffs could not bring federal cases before 1954","C":"That the NAACP concentrated on bus segregation from its founding","D":"That the courts were the only avenue closed to civil rights campaigners"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-1k-2"},{"id":"HIST-USA1-34","spec":"AQA-1K-USA1","stem":"Which distinction helps identify Harlem Renaissance?","options":{"A":"The 1898 conflict that ended Spanish rule in Cuba and expanded US influence","B":"A flowering of African American literature, music and art in the 1920s","C":"The movement of African Americans from the rural South to northern and western cities","D":"The US proposal that foreign powers should have equal trading access in China"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-HIST-USA1-34","scaffold":"Harlem Renaissance: A flowering of African American literature, music and art in the 1920s. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"Which interpretation of Harlem Renaissance is most accurate?","options":{"A":"It increased American global influence but also revived debate over isolationism","B":"It reflected search for work and safety and transformed urban politics and culture","C":"It marked a shift toward overseas empire and great-power diplomacy","D":"It challenged racist stereotypes and expressed new forms of cultural identity"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-1k-2"},{"id":"HIST-USA1-35","spec":"AQA-1K-USA1","stem":"A teacher asks for an accurate account of Spanish-American War. Which answer works?","options":{"A":"A flowering of African American literature, music and art in the 1920s","B":"The US proposal that foreign powers should have equal trading access in China","C":"The 1898 conflict that ended Spanish rule in Cuba and expanded US influence","D":"The claim that the USA could intervene in Latin America to prevent European involvement"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-HIST-USA1-35","scaffold":"Spanish-American War: The 1898 conflict that ended Spanish rule in Cuba and expanded US influence. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"What does a case involving Spanish-American War show?","options":{"A":"It marked a shift toward overseas empire and great-power diplomacy","B":"It challenged racist stereotypes and expressed new forms of cultural identity","C":"It sought commercial opportunity without formal partition of China","D":"It extended the Monroe Doctrine and justified a more assertive regional role"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-1k-2"},{"id":"HIST-USA1-36","spec":"AQA-1K-USA1","stem":"Which consequence can be linked to Open Door policy?","options":{"A":"The USA became an industrial and international power while racial inequality and political corruption persisted","B":"The claim that the USA could intervene in Latin America to prevent European involvement","C":"The 1898 conflict that ended Spanish rule in Cuba and expanded US influence","D":"The US proposal that foreign powers should have equal trading access in China"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-HIST-USA1-36","scaffold":"Open Door policy: The US proposal that foreign powers should have equal trading access in China. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"Which piece of evidence would best support an argument about Open Door policy?","options":{"A":"It marked a shift toward overseas empire and great-power diplomacy","B":"It sought commercial opportunity without formal partition of China","C":"It extended the Monroe Doctrine and justified a more assertive regional role","D":"It increased American global influence but also revived debate over isolationism"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-1k-2"},{"id":"HIST-USA1-37","spec":"AQA-1K-USA1","stem":"What does Roosevelt Corollary refer to?","options":{"A":"The claim that the USA could intervene in Latin America to prevent European involvement","B":"The USA entered the war in 1917 after neutrality became difficult to sustain","C":"The US proposal that foreign powers should have equal trading access in China","D":"The USA became an industrial and international power while racial inequality and political corruption persisted"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-HIST-USA1-37","scaffold":"Roosevelt Corollary: The claim that the USA could intervene in Latin America to prevent European involvement. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"Which example demonstrates Roosevelt Corollary?","options":{"A":"It increased American global influence but also revived debate over isolationism","B":"It sought commercial opportunity without formal partition of China","C":"It extended the Monroe Doctrine and justified a more assertive regional role","D":"It shows why constitutional change did not automatically produce racial equality"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-1k-2"},{"id":"HIST-USA1-38","spec":"AQA-1K-USA1","stem":"Which feature is most closely associated with First World War entry?","options":{"A":"Federal protection weakened as courts tolerated resistance","B":"The USA entered the war in 1917 after neutrality became difficult to sustain","C":"The claim that the USA could intervene in Latin America to prevent European involvement","D":"The USA became an industrial and international power while racial inequality and political corruption persisted"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-HIST-USA1-38","scaffold":"First World War entry: The USA entered the war in 1917 after neutrality became difficult to sustain. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"A student applies First World War entry to a case. Which judgement is strongest?","options":{"A":"It ended military conflict but left unresolved struggles over race, citizenship and federal power","B":"It extended the Monroe Doctrine and justified a more assertive regional role","C":"It shows why constitutional change did not automatically produce racial equality","D":"It increased American global influence but also revived debate over isolationism"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-1k-2"},{"id":"HIST-USA1-39","spec":"AQA-1K-USA1","stem":"A student is revising Fourteenth Amendment enforcement. Which description is accurate?","options":{"A":"The USA entered the war in 1917 after neutrality became difficult to sustain","B":"The USA became an industrial and international power while racial inequality and political corruption persisted","C":"Federal protection weakened as courts tolerated resistance","D":"The attempt to reunite the USA and define the status and rights of formerly enslaved people after the Civil War"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-HIST-RIGHTSDECLINE","scaffold":"Fourteenth Amendment enforcement: Federal protection weakened as courts tolerated resistance. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"What is the main significance of Fourteenth Amendment enforcement?","options":{"A":"It shows why constitutional change did not automatically produce racial equality","B":"It increased American global influence but also revived debate over isolationism","C":"Compare economic change against limits on freedom","D":"It ended military conflict but left unresolved struggles over race, citizenship and federal power"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-1k-2"},{"id":"HIST-USA1-40","spec":"AQA-1K-USA1","stem":"How should USA 1865–1920 judgement be defined in this course?","options":{"A":"A federal agency providing assistance and education to formerly enslaved people and poor Southerners","B":"The attempt to reunite the USA and define the status and rights of formerly enslaved people after the Civil War","C":"Federal protection weakened as courts tolerated resistance","D":"The USA became an industrial and international power while racial inequality and political corruption persisted"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-HIST-JUDGEMENT","scaffold":"USA 1865–1920 judgement: The USA became an industrial and international power while racial inequality and political corruption persisted. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"How should USA 1865–1920 judgement be evaluated?","options":{"A":"It shows why constitutional change did not automatically produce racial equality","B":"Compare economic change against limits on freedom","C":"It ended military conflict but left unresolved struggles over race, citizenship and federal power","D":"It created opportunities but was underfunded and faced resistance"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-1k-2"}]},"HIST-USA2":{"label":"USA 1920–1975","color":"#92400e","questions":[{"id":"HIST-USA2-01","spec":"AQA-1K-USA2","stem":"What does Roaring Twenties refer to?","options":{"A":"A period of consumer growth, cultural change and economic expansion during the 1920s","B":"Buying goods through instalments or borrowing rather than immediate payment","C":"The USA consolidated superpower status while experiencing deep racial, social and political conflict","D":"Numerical limits designed to reduce immigration, especially from southern and eastern Europe"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-HIST-USA2-1","scaffold":"Roaring Twenties: A period of consumer growth, cultural change and economic expansion during the 1920s. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"Which example demonstrates Roaring Twenties?","options":{"A":"It widened access to cars and appliances but made households and firms vulnerable to falling incomes","B":"A strong judgement weighs economic power and global leadership against inequality, war and distrust","C":"It improved living standards for some while leaving farmers, minorities and many workers vulnerable","D":"Federal troops showed that the national government could enforce civil-rights law against state resistance"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-1k-3"},{"id":"HIST-USA2-02","spec":"AQA-1K-USA2","stem":"Only about 2.5% of Americans owned shares in 1929. What does that imply about the Wall Street Crash?","options":{"A":"That the Crash had no economic consequences of any kind for the wider economy","B":"That the Crash alone cannot explain so vast a collapse","C":"That the Depression must have begun several years before the Crash took place","D":"That share ownership statistics from the period are unreliable and best set aside"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-HIST-CRASH","scaffold":"The Crash was an event of days; the Depression was a contraction from 1929 to 1933. With so few Americans holding shares, the mechanism has to be sought elsewhere: some 9,000 bank failures destroying deposits and credit, a money supply allowed to fall by roughly a third, debt deflation raising the real burden of what was owed, and collapsing world trade worsened by Smoot-Hawley. The Crash was the occasion and the confidence shock; the banking system's failure was the cause.","reforge":{"stem":"Which factor did most to turn the 1929 downturn into a decade-long depression?","options":{"A":"The introduction of the Smoot-Hawley Tariff in the summer of 1930","B":"The suspension of trading on the New York Stock Exchange in 1929","C":"Bank failures and monetary contraction","D":"The refusal of European governments to repay their wartime debts"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-1k-3"},{"id":"HIST-USA2-03","spec":"AQA-1K-USA2","stem":"A student is revising prohibition. Which description is accurate?","options":{"A":"Buying goods through instalments or borrowing rather than immediate payment","B":"Fear of communist revolution and radical subversion after the First World War","C":"The constitutional ban on the manufacture, sale and transport of alcohol","D":"Numerical limits designed to reduce immigration, especially from southern and eastern Europe"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-HIST-USA2-3","scaffold":"prohibition: The constitutional ban on the manufacture, sale and transport of alcohol. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"What is the main significance of prohibition?","options":{"A":"It encouraged illegal supply and organised crime while failing to remove demand","B":"It widened access to cars and appliances but made households and firms vulnerable to falling incomes","C":"It encouraged repression and suspicion of immigrants, trade unionists and dissenters","D":"They reflected nativism and racial assumptions about who belonged in the nation"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-1k-3"},{"id":"HIST-USA2-04","spec":"AQA-1K-USA2","stem":"How should Red Scare be defined in this course?","options":{"A":"The severe economic crisis of the 1930s marked by unemployment, bank failures and falling output","B":"Numerical limits designed to reduce immigration, especially from southern and eastern Europe","C":"The constitutional ban on the manufacture, sale and transport of alcohol","D":"Fear of communist revolution and radical subversion after the First World War"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-HIST-USA2-4","scaffold":"Red Scare: Fear of communist revolution and radical subversion after the First World War. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"How should Red Scare be evaluated?","options":{"A":"It encouraged illegal supply and organised crime while failing to remove demand","B":"It encouraged repression and suspicion of immigrants, trade unionists and dissenters","C":"They reflected nativism and racial assumptions about who belonged in the nation","D":"It appeared inadequate as the crisis deepened and contributed to Hoover's unpopularity"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-1k-3"},{"id":"HIST-USA2-05","spec":"AQA-1K-USA2","stem":"Which example best illustrates immigration quotas?","options":{"A":"Numerical limits designed to reduce immigration, especially from southern and eastern Europe","B":"The sharp collapse in US share prices in October 1929","C":"Fear of communist revolution and radical subversion after the First World War","D":"The severe economic crisis of the 1930s marked by unemployment, bank failures and falling output"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-HIST-USA2-5","scaffold":"immigration quotas: Numerical limits designed to reduce immigration, especially from southern and eastern Europe. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"What is the defensible view of immigration quotas?","options":{"A":"It damaged confidence and exposed the weaknesses of speculation and credit","B":"It encouraged repression and suspicion of immigrants, trade unionists and dissenters","C":"They reflected nativism and racial assumptions about who belonged in the nation","D":"It undermined faith in laissez-faire and expanded expectations of federal action"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-1k-3"},{"id":"HIST-USA2-06","spec":"AQA-1K-USA2","stem":"What role does Wall Street Crash play in the topic?","options":{"A":"The severe economic crisis of the 1930s marked by unemployment, bank failures and falling output","B":"The sharp collapse in US share prices in October 1929","C":"Numerical limits designed to reduce immigration, especially from southern and eastern Europe","D":"The belief that government should encourage voluntary cooperation rather than direct relief"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-HIST-CRASH","scaffold":"Wall Street Crash: The sharp collapse in US share prices in October 1929. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"In a comparison question, what should a student say about Wall Street Crash?","options":{"A":"It appeared inadequate as the crisis deepened and contributed to Hoover's unpopularity","B":"They reflected nativism and racial assumptions about who belonged in the nation","C":"It undermined faith in laissez-faire and expanded expectations of federal action","D":"It damaged confidence and exposed the weaknesses of speculation and credit"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-1k-3"},{"id":"HIST-USA2-07","spec":"AQA-1K-USA2","stem":"Why is Great Depression significant?","options":{"A":"The sharp collapse in US share prices in October 1929","B":"The belief that government should encourage voluntary cooperation rather than direct relief","C":"The severe economic crisis of the 1930s marked by unemployment, bank failures and falling output","D":"Criticism from conservatives, businessmen, the Supreme Court and those who wanted more radical action"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-HIST-CRASH","scaffold":"Great Depression: The severe economic crisis of the 1930s marked by unemployment, bank failures and falling output. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"Which outcome is most closely connected with Great Depression?","options":{"A":"It undermined faith in laissez-faire and expanded expectations of federal action","B":"It damaged confidence and exposed the weaknesses of speculation and credit","C":"It appeared inadequate as the crisis deepened and contributed to Hoover's unpopularity","D":"It expanded federal responsibility without fully ending unemployment before wartime mobilisation"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-1k-3"},{"id":"HIST-USA2-08","spec":"AQA-1K-USA2","stem":"Which feature of the New Deal is hardest to reconcile with calling it a single coherent plan?","options":{"A":"That it was introduced by a president who had campaigned on a balanced budget","B":"That its agencies were known by initials rather than by their full legal titles","C":"That the AAA destroyed crops while the NRA suspended antitrust","D":"That it required a large expansion in the number of federal government employees"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-HIST-NEWDEAL-PLAN","scaffold":"The First New Deal (1933–34) worked through cooperation with business — AAA, NRA, CCC. The Second (1935–36) turned to rights and permanent structures — Wagner Act, Social Security, WPA. The AAA paid farmers to destroy output to raise prices; the NRA suspended antitrust to let industries fix prices, the opposite of the trust-busting tradition. Schechter (1935) struck down the NRA and forced the shift. Roosevelt himself called for 'bold, persistent experimentation'.","reforge":{"stem":"What forced the change of direction between the First and Second New Deals?","options":{"A":"The Court striking down the NRA, and pressure from Long","B":"The Republican victory in the 1934 midterm elections to Congress","C":"Roosevelt's decision to balance the federal budget from 1935 onwards","D":"The outbreak of war in Europe and the shift to military production"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-1k-3"},{"id":"HIST-USA2-09","spec":"AQA-1K-USA2","stem":"How does New Deal affect the system or period being studied?","options":{"A":"Franklin Roosevelt's programme of relief, recovery and reform after 1933","B":"The temporary closure and inspection of banks ordered by Roosevelt in 1933","C":"The belief that government should encourage voluntary cooperation rather than direct relief","D":"The rapid legislative programme passed during Roosevelt's opening months in office"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-HIST-NEWDEAL-PLAN","scaffold":"New Deal: Franklin Roosevelt's programme of relief, recovery and reform after 1933. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"Why might New Deal matter in this context?","options":{"A":"It helped restore confidence and demonstrated decisive presidential action","B":"It appeared inadequate as the crisis deepened and contributed to Hoover's unpopularity","C":"It expanded federal responsibility without fully ending unemployment before wartime mobilisation","D":"It linked wartime service to demands for civil rights and exposed contradictions in US democracy"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-1k-3"},{"id":"HIST-USA2-10","spec":"AQA-1K-USA2","stem":"Which distinction helps identify bank holiday?","options":{"A":"The rapid legislative programme passed during Roosevelt's opening months in office","B":"The temporary closure and inspection of banks ordered by Roosevelt in 1933","C":"Franklin Roosevelt's programme of relief, recovery and reform after 1933","D":"Radio addresses in which Roosevelt explained policy directly to the public"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-HIST-NEWDEAL-PLAN","scaffold":"bank holiday: The temporary closure and inspection of banks ordered by Roosevelt in 1933. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"Which interpretation of bank holiday is most accurate?","options":{"A":"They made federal action visible but increased bureaucracy and provoked criticism","B":"It expanded federal responsibility without fully ending unemployment before wartime mobilisation","C":"It created key agencies and showed the strength of executive leadership in crisis","D":"It helped restore confidence and demonstrated decisive presidential action"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-1k-3"},{"id":"HIST-USA2-11","spec":"AQA-1K-USA2","stem":"A teacher asks for an accurate account of First Hundred Days. Which answer works?","options":{"A":"The temporary closure and inspection of banks ordered by Roosevelt in 1933","B":"New Deal agencies created to regulate, employ and provide relief","C":"The rapid legislative programme passed during Roosevelt's opening months in office","D":"A law providing pensions and limited support for unemployment and vulnerable groups"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-HIST-NEWDEAL-PLAN","scaffold":"First Hundred Days: The rapid legislative programme passed during Roosevelt's opening months in office. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"What does a case involving First Hundred Days show?","options":{"A":"It created key agencies and showed the strength of executive leadership in crisis","B":"It helped restore confidence and demonstrated decisive presidential action","C":"They made federal action visible but increased bureaucracy and provoked criticism","D":"It established a lasting federal welfare role but excluded or disadvantaged some workers"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-1k-3"},{"id":"HIST-USA2-12","spec":"AQA-1K-USA2","stem":"Which consequence can be linked to Alphabet Agencies?","options":{"A":"A law providing pensions and limited support for unemployment and vulnerable groups","B":"Radio addresses in which Roosevelt explained policy directly to the public","C":"The rapid legislative programme passed during Roosevelt's opening months in office","D":"New Deal agencies created to regulate, employ and provide relief"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-HIST-NEWDEAL-PLAN","scaffold":"Alphabet Agencies: New Deal agencies created to regulate, employ and provide relief. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"Which piece of evidence would best support an argument about Alphabet Agencies?","options":{"A":"It created key agencies and showed the strength of executive leadership in crisis","B":"They made federal action visible but increased bureaucracy and provoked criticism","C":"They strengthened presidential communication and helped create confidence","D":"It established a lasting federal welfare role but excluded or disadvantaged some workers"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-1k-3"},{"id":"HIST-USA2-13","spec":"AQA-1K-USA2","stem":"What does fireside chats refer to?","options":{"A":"Radio addresses in which Roosevelt explained policy directly to the public","B":"A law providing pensions and limited support for unemployment and vulnerable groups","C":"New Deal agencies created to regulate, employ and provide relief","D":"The 1935 law protecting collective bargaining and creating the National Labor Relations Board"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-HIST-NEWDEAL-PLAN","scaffold":"fireside chats: Radio addresses in which Roosevelt explained policy directly to the public. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"Which example demonstrates fireside chats?","options":{"A":"It established a lasting federal welfare role but excluded or disadvantaged some workers","B":"They made federal action visible but increased bureaucracy and provoked criticism","C":"They strengthened presidential communication and helped create confidence","D":"It strengthened organised labour and shifted the balance between workers and employers"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-1k-3"},{"id":"HIST-USA2-14","spec":"AQA-1K-USA2","stem":"Which feature is most closely associated with Social Security Act 1935?","options":{"A":"The 1935 law protecting collective bargaining and creating the National Labor Relations Board","B":"A law providing pensions and limited support for unemployment and vulnerable groups","C":"Radio addresses in which Roosevelt explained policy directly to the public","D":"Criticism from conservatives, businessmen, the Supreme Court and those who wanted more radical action"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-HIST-NEWDEAL-PLAN","scaffold":"Social Security Act 1935: A law providing pensions and limited support for unemployment and vulnerable groups. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"A student applies Social Security Act 1935 to a case. Which judgement is strongest?","options":{"A":"It linked wartime service to demands for civil rights and exposed contradictions in US democracy","B":"They strengthened presidential communication and helped create confidence","C":"It strengthened organised labour and shifted the balance between workers and employers","D":"It established a lasting federal welfare role but excluded or disadvantaged some workers"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-1k-3"},{"id":"HIST-USA2-15","spec":"AQA-1K-USA2","stem":"A student is revising Wagner Act. Which description is accurate?","options":{"A":"A law providing pensions and limited support for unemployment and vulnerable groups","B":"Criticism from conservatives, businessmen, the Supreme Court and those who wanted more radical action","C":"The 1935 law protecting collective bargaining and creating the National Labor Relations Board","D":"The USA consolidated superpower status while experiencing deep racial, social and political conflict"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-HIST-NEWDEAL-PLAN","scaffold":"Wagner Act: The 1935 law protecting collective bargaining and creating the National Labor Relations Board. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"What is the main significance of Wagner Act?","options":{"A":"It strengthened organised labour and shifted the balance between workers and employers","B":"It established a lasting federal welfare role but excluded or disadvantaged some workers","C":"It shows that the New Deal was contested rather than universally accepted","D":"It linked wartime service to demands for civil rights and exposed contradictions in US democracy"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-1k-3"},{"id":"HIST-USA2-16","spec":"AQA-1K-USA2","stem":"Huey Long, Father Coughlin and Dr Townsend attacked the New Deal. On what grounds?","options":{"A":"That it was unconstitutional and exceeded the powers granted to the federal government","B":"That it favoured industrial workers in the North at the expense of Southern farmers","C":"That it would provoke retaliation from foreign governments and damage American trade","D":"That it did too little, and wealth should be redistributed far more aggressively"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-HIST-NDOPP","scaffold":"Opposition came from BOTH directions. From the right: the Liberty League, business, and the Supreme Court on constitutional grounds in Schechter and Butler. From the LEFT: Long's Share Our Wealth promising $5,000 a family, Coughlin's radio audience, Townsend's $200-a-month pension clubs. The Second New Deal — Social Security, the Wealth Tax Act — was partly a response to that pressure. Roosevelt moved left because the electoral threat was coming from his left.","reforge":{"stem":"On what basis did the Supreme Court strike down the NRA in Schechter (1935)?","options":{"A":"That its codes discriminated against small businesses and family firms","B":"That the President had failed to obtain congressional approval for it","C":"That its provisions violated the rights guaranteed by the Fifth Amendment","D":"That it delegated legislative power and over-reached the commerce clause"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-1k-3"},{"id":"HIST-USA2-17","spec":"AQA-1K-USA2","stem":"Which example best illustrates court-packing plan?","options":{"A":"Roosevelt's 1937 proposal to add justices to the Supreme Court","B":"The rapid expansion of production and military employment after US entry into the war","C":"Criticism from conservatives, businessmen, the Supreme Court and those who wanted more radical action","D":"The campaign for victory against fascism abroad and racism at home"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-HIST-NDOPP","scaffold":"court-packing plan: Roosevelt's 1937 proposal to add justices to the Supreme Court. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"What is the defensible view of court-packing plan?","options":{"A":"It ended mass unemployment and made the federal government central to the economy","B":"It shows that the New Deal was contested rather than universally accepted","C":"It failed politically and revealed limits to presidential power","D":"It linked wartime service to demands for civil rights and exposed contradictions in US democracy"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-1k-3"},{"id":"HIST-USA2-18","spec":"AQA-1K-USA2","stem":"US unemployment fell to 14.3% by 1937 but rose again to 19.0% in 1938. What does that show?","options":{"A":"That recovery was not self-sustaining once federal spending was withdrawn","B":"That the New Deal had ended the Depression by 1937 as Roosevelt claimed","C":"That unemployment statistics before the war were compiled unreliably","D":"That the Wagner Act had priced large numbers of workers out of employment"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-HIST-NEWDEAL-END","scaffold":"The 1937–38 recession followed Roosevelt cutting spending in the belief recovery was secured, while new Social Security payroll taxes withdrew purchasing power. Unemployment rose nearly five points in a year. That bump is the decisive evidence that the New Deal had not ended the Depression: pre-1929 levels are not reached until war production, around 1941–42. Relief, recovery and reform are three different claims, and the record is far stronger on the first and third.","reforge":{"stem":"What finally returned the United States to full employment?","options":{"A":"The Works Progress Administration's expansion in the later 1930s","B":"Wartime production and military mobilisation after 1941","C":"The Social Security Act's payments reaching retirees from 1940","D":"The devaluation of the dollar against gold in 1933 and 1934"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-1k-3"},{"id":"HIST-USA2-19","spec":"AQA-1K-USA2","stem":"Why is Double V campaign significant?","options":{"A":"The rapid expansion of production and military employment after US entry into the war","B":"The 1947 policy of supporting states resisting perceived communist expansion","C":"The campaign for victory against fascism abroad and racism at home","D":"The use of accusations of communist subversion to gain political influence"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-HIST-CIVILRIGHTS-START","scaffold":"Double V campaign: The campaign for victory against fascism abroad and racism at home. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"Which outcome is most closely connected with Double V campaign?","options":{"A":"It linked wartime service to demands for civil rights and exposed contradictions in US democracy","B":"It ended mass unemployment and made the federal government central to the economy","C":"It formalised containment and made the USA a leading power in the Cold War","D":"Federal troops showed that the national government could enforce civil-rights law against state resistance"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-1k-3"},{"id":"HIST-USA2-20","spec":"AQA-1K-USA2","stem":"What distinguishes the Truman Doctrine from the Marshall Plan and NATO?","options":{"A":"It was the only one of the three that was formally ratified by the US Senate","B":"It stated a principle; the others were money and a treaty","C":"It was directed at Asia, while the other two applied only to Western Europe","D":"It was a temporary measure, while the other two created permanent institutions"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-HIST-COLDWARPOLICY","scaffold":"Truman Doctrine, March 1947: a declared PRINCIPLE — support for free peoples resisting subjugation — attached to $400m for Greece and Turkey. Marshall Plan, 1948–52: an ECONOMIC programme, roughly $13bn to sixteen countries, formally offered to the USSR and refused. NATO, April 1949: a binding MILITARY alliance with Article 5. Each escalates the kind of commitment, from statement to money to treaty obligation — which is what Kennan spent years objecting to.","reforge":{"stem":"Why did Kennan, whose Long Telegram supplied the analysis, object to the Truman Doctrine?","options":{"A":"He believed the Soviet Union posed no threat to Western European security","B":"He had argued for containment through the United Nations rather than unilaterally","C":"He wanted resistance at key industrial points, not everywhere","D":"He thought Greece and Turkey were already beyond any realistic American help"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-1k-4"},{"id":"HIST-USA2-21","spec":"AQA-1K-USA2","stem":"How does Marshall Plan affect the system or period being studied?","options":{"A":"US economic aid for European recovery after 1945","B":"The North Atlantic Treaty Organisation formed in 1949","C":"The 1947 policy of supporting states resisting perceived communist expansion","D":"The use of accusations of communist subversion to gain political influence"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-HIST-COLDWARPOLICY","scaffold":"Marshall Plan: US economic aid for European recovery after 1945. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"Why might Marshall Plan matter in this context?","options":{"A":"It created a permanent collective-security alliance linking the USA to western Europe","B":"It formalised containment and made the USA a leading power in the Cold War","C":"It combined humanitarian reconstruction with the strategic aim of limiting communist influence","D":"Federal troops showed that the national government could enforce civil-rights law against state resistance"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-1k-4"},{"id":"HIST-USA2-22","spec":"AQA-1K-USA2","stem":"Which distinction helps identify NATO?","options":{"A":"The use of accusations of communist subversion to gain political influence","B":"The North Atlantic Treaty Organisation formed in 1949","C":"US economic aid for European recovery after 1945","D":"The 1950–53 conflict in which the USA led a UN force against North Korean and Chinese forces"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-HIST-COLDWARPOLICY","scaffold":"NATO: The North Atlantic Treaty Organisation formed in 1949. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"Which interpretation of NATO is most accurate?","options":{"A":"It showed the global and military nature of containment without producing reunification","B":"It combined humanitarian reconstruction with the strategic aim of limiting communist influence","C":"It damaged civil liberties and careers while exploiting Cold War fears","D":"It created a permanent collective-security alliance linking the USA to western Europe"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-1k-4"},{"id":"HIST-USA2-23","spec":"AQA-1K-USA2","stem":"A teacher asks for an accurate account of McCarthyism. Which answer works?","options":{"A":"The North Atlantic Treaty Organisation formed in 1949","B":"The 1950–53 conflict in which the USA led a UN force against North Korean and Chinese forces","C":"The use of accusations of communist subversion to gain political influence","D":"The 1954 Supreme Court ruling that segregated public schools were unconstitutional"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-HIST-USA2-23","scaffold":"McCarthyism: The use of accusations of communist subversion to gain political influence. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"What does a case involving McCarthyism show?","options":{"A":"It damaged civil liberties and careers while exploiting Cold War fears","B":"It created a permanent collective-security alliance linking the USA to western Europe","C":"It showed the global and military nature of containment without producing reunification","D":"It reflected prosperity and car ownership but reinforced racial and class segregation"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-1k-4"},{"id":"HIST-USA2-24","spec":"AQA-1K-USA2","stem":"Which consequence can be linked to Korean War?","options":{"A":"The USA consolidated superpower status while experiencing deep racial, social and political conflict","B":"The movement of many Americans into expanding suburbs after 1945","C":"The use of accusations of communist subversion to gain political influence","D":"The 1950–53 conflict in which the USA led a UN force against North Korean and Chinese forces"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-HIST-COLDWARPOLICY","scaffold":"Korean War: The 1950–53 conflict in which the USA led a UN force against North Korean and Chinese forces. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"Which piece of evidence would best support an argument about Korean War?","options":{"A":"It damaged civil liberties and careers while exploiting Cold War fears","B":"It showed the global and military nature of containment without producing reunification","C":"It reflected prosperity and car ownership but reinforced racial and class segregation","D":"It reshaped demand for housing, schools and services and influenced later youth politics"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-1k-4"},{"id":"HIST-USA2-25","spec":"AQA-1K-USA2","stem":"What does suburbanisation refer to?","options":{"A":"The movement of many Americans into expanding suburbs after 1945","B":"The rise in births after the Second World War","C":"The 1950–53 conflict in which the USA led a UN force against North Korean and Chinese forces","D":"The 1954 Supreme Court ruling that segregated public schools were unconstitutional"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-HIST-USA2-25","scaffold":"suburbanisation: The movement of many Americans into expanding suburbs after 1945. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"Which example demonstrates suburbanisation?","options":{"A":"It reshaped demand for housing, schools and services and influenced later youth politics","B":"It showed the global and military nature of containment without producing reunification","C":"It reflected prosperity and car ownership but reinforced racial and class segregation","D":"It overturned the legal basis of school segregation but required sustained enforcement"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-1k-4"},{"id":"HIST-USA2-26","spec":"AQA-1K-USA2","stem":"Which feature is most closely associated with Baby Boom?","options":{"A":"The campaign against racial segregation and discrimination","B":"The rise in births after the Second World War","C":"The movement of many Americans into expanding suburbs after 1945","D":"The 1954 Supreme Court ruling that segregated public schools were unconstitutional"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-HIST-USA2-26","scaffold":"Baby Boom: The rise in births after the Second World War. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"A student applies Baby Boom to a case. Which judgement is strongest?","options":{"A":"Federal troops showed that the national government could enforce civil-rights law against state resistance","B":"It reflected prosperity and car ownership but reinforced racial and class segregation","C":"It combined legal action, protest and federal intervention to challenge Jim Crow","D":"It reshaped demand for housing, schools and services and influenced later youth politics"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-1k-4"},{"id":"HIST-USA2-27","spec":"AQA-1K-USA2","stem":"Ten years after Brown v Board, roughly what share of black students in the South attended desegregated schools?","options":{"A":"About 60%, following steady compliance by Southern school boards after 1955","B":"About 30%, with the remainder concentrated in the states of the Deep South","C":"About 2%, because the ruling set no deadline and enforcement was resisted","D":"None at all, since no Southern district admitted a black student before 1964"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-HIST-CIVILRIGHTS-LAW","scaffold":"Brown II (1955) required desegregation with 'all deliberate speed' — a phrase containing no DEADLINE, which handed the pace to states with no intention of moving. 101 congressmen signed the Southern Manifesto in 1956; Little Rock in 1957 took the 101st Airborne for one school. What moved the figure was money and machinery: Title VI of the 1964 Act allowed federal funding to be withdrawn, and Green (1968) required plans that worked. A ruling is a permission to litigate, not a change on the ground.","reforge":{"stem":"Which mechanism did most to enforce school desegregation in practice?","options":{"A":"The threat of withdrawing federal education funding under the 1964 Act","B":"The deployment of federal troops to individual schools across the South","C":"The Supreme Court's decision to set a firm compliance date in Brown II","D":"State legislatures voluntarily repealing their own segregation statutes"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-1k-4"},{"id":"HIST-USA2-28","spec":"AQA-1K-USA2","stem":"How should Brown v Board of Education be defined in this course?","options":{"A":"Federal legislation prohibiting discrimination in public accommodations and employment","B":"The 1955–56 protest against segregated buses in Montgomery, Alabama","C":"The campaign against racial segregation and discrimination","D":"The 1954 Supreme Court ruling that segregated public schools were unconstitutional"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-HIST-CIVILRIGHTS-LAW","scaffold":"Brown v Board of Education: The 1954 Supreme Court ruling that segregated public schools were unconstitutional. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"How should Brown v Board of Education be evaluated?","options":{"A":"It combined legal action, protest and federal intervention to challenge Jim Crow","B":"It overturned the legal basis of school segregation but required sustained enforcement","C":"It demonstrated the power of sustained local organisation and economic pressure","D":"Federal troops showed that the national government could enforce civil-rights law against state resistance"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-1k-4"},{"id":"HIST-USA2-29","spec":"AQA-1K-USA2","stem":"Which example best illustrates Montgomery Bus Boycott?","options":{"A":"The 1955–56 protest against segregated buses in Montgomery, Alabama","B":"The 1957 crisis over the integration of Central High School in Arkansas","C":"The 1954 Supreme Court ruling that segregated public schools were unconstitutional","D":"Federal legislation prohibiting discrimination in public accommodations and employment"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-HIST-CIVILRIGHTS-LAW","scaffold":"Montgomery Bus Boycott: The 1955–56 protest against segregated buses in Montgomery, Alabama. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"What is the defensible view of Montgomery Bus Boycott?","options":{"A":"Federal troops showed that the national government could enforce civil-rights law against state resistance","B":"It overturned the legal basis of school segregation but required sustained enforcement","C":"It demonstrated the power of sustained local organisation and economic pressure","D":"It represented a major legal victory but could not alone remove economic inequality"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-1k-4"},{"id":"HIST-USA2-30","spec":"AQA-1K-USA2","stem":"What role does Little Rock play in the topic?","options":{"A":"Federal legislation prohibiting discrimination in public accommodations and employment","B":"The 1957 crisis over the integration of Central High School in Arkansas","C":"The 1955–56 protest against segregated buses in Montgomery, Alabama","D":"Lyndon Johnson's programme of welfare, education and civil-rights reform"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-HIST-CIVILRIGHTS-LAW","scaffold":"Little Rock: The 1957 crisis over the integration of Central High School in Arkansas. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"In a comparison question, what should a student say about Little Rock?","options":{"A":"It damaged public trust and demonstrated that presidential power remained subject to constitutional checks","B":"It demonstrated the power of sustained local organisation and economic pressure","C":"It represented a major legal victory but could not alone remove economic inequality","D":"Federal troops showed that the national government could enforce civil-rights law against state resistance"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-1k-4"},{"id":"HIST-USA2-31","spec":"AQA-1K-USA2","stem":"Why is Civil Rights Act 1964 significant?","options":{"A":"The 1957 crisis over the integration of Central High School in Arkansas","B":"Federal legislation protecting African American voting rights","C":"Federal legislation prohibiting discrimination in public accommodations and employment","D":"A conservative movement opposing big government, liberal social change and high taxation"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-HIST-CIVILRIGHTS-LAW","scaffold":"Civil Rights Act 1964: Federal legislation prohibiting discrimination in public accommodations and employment. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"Which outcome is most closely connected with Civil Rights Act 1964?","options":{"A":"It represented a major legal victory but could not alone remove economic inequality","B":"A period of consumer growth, cultural change and economic expansion during the 1920s","C":"It sharply increased registration and participation by attacking discriminatory practices","D":"It reduced some forms of poverty but faced cost, political opposition and structural inequality"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-1k-4"},{"id":"HIST-USA2-32","spec":"AQA-1K-USA2","stem":"What is the supported by the evidence view of Voting Rights Act 1965?","options":{"A":"The prolonged US intervention against communist forces in Vietnam","B":"Lyndon Johnson's programme of welfare, education and civil-rights reform","C":"Federal legislation prohibiting discrimination in public accommodations and employment","D":"Federal legislation protecting African American voting rights"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-HIST-CIVILRIGHTS-LAW","scaffold":"Voting Rights Act 1965: Federal legislation protecting African American voting rights. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"A historian or political analyst considers Voting Rights Act 1965. Which conclusion is sound?","options":{"A":"It represented a major legal victory but could not alone remove economic inequality","B":"It sharply increased registration and participation by attacking discriminatory practices","C":"It reduced some forms of poverty but faced cost, political opposition and structural inequality","D":"It reflected backlash against the Great Society and helped reshape later Republican politics"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-1k-4"},{"id":"HIST-USA2-33","spec":"AQA-1K-USA2","stem":"How does Great Society affect the system or period being studied?","options":{"A":"Lyndon Johnson's programme of welfare, education and civil-rights reform","B":"The prolonged US intervention against communist forces in Vietnam","C":"Federal legislation protecting African American voting rights","D":"A conservative movement opposing big government, liberal social change and high taxation"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-HIST-USA2-33","scaffold":"Great Society: Lyndon Johnson's programme of welfare, education and civil-rights reform. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"Why might Great Society matter in this context?","options":{"A":"It weakened confidence in government and intensified domestic protest and division","B":"It sharply increased registration and participation by attacking discriminatory practices","C":"It reduced some forms of poverty but faced cost, political opposition and structural inequality","D":"It damaged public trust and demonstrated that presidential power remained subject to constitutional checks"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-1k-4"},{"id":"HIST-USA2-34","spec":"AQA-1K-USA2","stem":"Which distinction helps identify Vietnam War?","options":{"A":"A conservative movement opposing big government, liberal social change and high taxation","B":"The prolonged US intervention against communist forces in Vietnam","C":"Lyndon Johnson's programme of welfare, education and civil-rights reform","D":"A period of reduced tension between the USA and USSR during the 1970s"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-HIST-USA2-34","scaffold":"Vietnam War: The prolonged US intervention against communist forces in Vietnam. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"Which interpretation of Vietnam War is most accurate?","options":{"A":"It damaged public trust and demonstrated that presidential power remained subject to constitutional checks","B":"It reduced some forms of poverty but faced cost, political opposition and structural inequality","C":"It reflected backlash against the Great Society and helped reshape later Republican politics","D":"It weakened confidence in government and intensified domestic protest and division"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-1k-4"},{"id":"HIST-USA2-35","spec":"AQA-1K-USA2","stem":"A teacher asks for an accurate account of New Right. Which answer works?","options":{"A":"The prolonged US intervention against communist forces in Vietnam","B":"The political scandal that led to Nixon's resignation in 1974","C":"A conservative movement opposing big government, liberal social change and high taxation","D":"The USA consolidated superpower status while experiencing deep racial, social and political conflict"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-HIST-USA2-35","scaffold":"New Right: A conservative movement opposing big government, liberal social change and high taxation. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"What does a case involving New Right show?","options":{"A":"It reflected backlash against the Great Society and helped reshape later Republican politics","B":"It weakened confidence in government and intensified domestic protest and division","C":"It damaged public trust and demonstrated that presidential power remained subject to constitutional checks","D":"They expanded opportunity but provoked debate over equality, merit and the role of government"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-1k-4"},{"id":"HIST-USA2-36","spec":"AQA-1K-USA2","stem":"Which consequence can be linked to Watergate?","options":{"A":"Policies intended to improve access for groups disadvantaged by historic discrimination","B":"A period of reduced tension between the USA and USSR during the 1970s","C":"A conservative movement opposing big government, liberal social change and high taxation","D":"The political scandal that led to Nixon's resignation in 1974"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-HIST-USA2-36","scaffold":"Watergate: The political scandal that led to Nixon's resignation in 1974. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"Which piece of evidence would best support an argument about Watergate?","options":{"A":"It reflected backlash against the Great Society and helped reshape later Republican politics","B":"It damaged public trust and demonstrated that presidential power remained subject to constitutional checks","C":"It reflected strategic calculation but did not end competition or ideological rivalry","D":"It reduced public spending but caused resentment because families felt their privacy and dignity were invaded"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-1k-4"},{"id":"HIST-USA2-37","spec":"AQA-1K-USA2","stem":"What does detente refer to?","options":{"A":"A period of reduced tension between the USA and USSR during the 1970s","B":"Policies intended to improve access for groups disadvantaged by historic discrimination","C":"The political scandal that led to Nixon's resignation in 1974","D":"The USA consolidated superpower status while experiencing deep racial, social and political conflict"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-HIST-COLDWARPOLICY","scaffold":"detente: A period of reduced tension between the USA and USSR during the 1970s. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"Which example demonstrates detente?","options":{"A":"They expanded opportunity but provoked debate over equality, merit and the role of government","B":"It damaged public trust and demonstrated that presidential power remained subject to constitutional checks","C":"It reflected strategic calculation but did not end competition or ideological rivalry","D":"A strong judgement weighs economic power and global leadership against inequality, war and distrust"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-1k-4"},{"id":"HIST-USA2-38","spec":"AQA-1K-USA2","stem":"Which feature is most closely associated with affirmative action?","options":{"A":"The USA consolidated superpower status while experiencing deep racial, social and political conflict","B":"Policies intended to improve access for groups disadvantaged by historic discrimination","C":"A period of reduced tension between the USA and USSR during the 1970s","D":"Numerical limits designed to reduce immigration, especially from southern and eastern Europe"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-HIST-USA2-38","scaffold":"affirmative action: Policies intended to improve access for groups disadvantaged by historic discrimination. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"A student applies affirmative action to a case. Which judgement is strongest?","options":{"A":"It improved living standards for some while leaving farmers, minorities and many workers vulnerable","B":"It reflected strategic calculation but did not end competition or ideological rivalry","C":"A strong judgement weighs economic power and global leadership against inequality, war and distrust","D":"They expanded opportunity but provoked debate over equality, merit and the role of government"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-1k-4"},{"id":"HIST-USA2-39","spec":"AQA-1K-USA2","stem":"A student is revising USA 1920–1975 judgement. Which description is accurate?","options":{"A":"Policies intended to improve access for groups disadvantaged by historic discrimination","B":"A period of consumer growth, cultural change and economic expansion during the 1920s","C":"The USA consolidated superpower status while experiencing deep racial, social and political conflict","D":"Criticism from conservatives, businessmen, the Supreme Court and those who wanted more radical action"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-HIST-JUDGEMENT","scaffold":"USA 1920–1975 judgement: The USA consolidated superpower status while experiencing deep racial, social and political conflict. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"What is the main significance of USA 1920–1975 judgement?","options":{"A":"A strong judgement weighs economic power and global leadership against inequality, war and distrust","B":"They expanded opportunity but provoked debate over equality, merit and the role of government","C":"It improved living standards for some while leaving farmers, minorities and many workers vulnerable","D":"It widened access to cars and appliances but made households and firms vulnerable to falling incomes"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-1k-4"},{"id":"HIST-USA2-40","spec":"AQA-1K-USA2","tag":"MC-HIST-NEWDEAL-END","specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-1k-3","stem":"Which option about the New Deal is best supported by the unemployment figures?","options":{"A":"It ended the Depression within Roosevelt's first hundred days in office","B":"It had no measurable effect on unemployment at any point in the 1930s","C":"It cut unemployment substantially without restoring full employment","D":"It raised unemployment every year until the outbreak of war in Europe"},"correct":"C","scaffold":"Unemployment ran at 24.9% in 1933, 14.3% by 1937, back up to 19.0% in 1938 after spending was cut, then 9.9% in 1941 and 1.2% by 1944. So the New Deal achieved a large reduction and did not restore full employment; war production did. Distinguish the three claims made for it — relief, recovery, reform — because the record is much stronger on relief and reform than on recovery.","reforge":{"stem":"Why is the 1937–38 recession important when judging the New Deal?","options":{"A":"It proved that the Supreme Court had been right to strike down the NRA","B":"It was caused by the Wagner Act raising wage costs across industry","C":"It showed recovery collapsed as soon as federal stimulus was withdrawn","D":"It marked the point at which unemployment first fell below 10%"},"correct":"C"}}]},"HIST-TUDOR":{"label":"Tudor Historical Investigation","color":"#92400e","questions":[{"id":"HIST-TUDOR-01","spec":"AQA-NEA-TUDOR","stem":"Why did Henry VII date the start of his reign to the day BEFORE Bosworth?","options":{"A":"To ensure that Richard III's grants of land and title remained legally valid","B":"Because the traditional practice was to date a reign from the previous monarch's death","C":"To establish that he held the crown through his marriage to Elizabeth of York","D":"To make everyone who fought for Richard III a traitor by legal definition"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-HIST-TUDORCLAIM","scaffold":"Henry's hereditary claim was weak: through Margaret Beaufort, from John of Gaunt's third marriage, legitimated in 1397 but expressly barred from the crown by Henry IV's 1407 patent; his father's line brought no royal blood at all. So the title rested on victory, marriage and consolidation. Two acts reveal he knew it: backdating the reign, and being crowned in October 1485 but marrying Elizabeth of York only in January 1486 — establishing that he held the crown in his own right, not through his wife.","reforge":{"stem":"Why did Henry VII delay his marriage to Elizabeth of York until after his coronation?","options":{"A":"Because a papal dispensation for the marriage did not arrive until 1486","B":"To establish that he held the crown in his own right and not through her","C":"Because Parliament had not yet confirmed Elizabeth's own claim to the throne","D":"To allow time for the Yorkist claimants to be defeated at Stoke Field first"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-nea-content"},{"id":"HIST-TUDOR-02","spec":"AQA-NEA-TUDOR","stem":"Which feature is most closely associated with Henry VII's claim?","options":{"A":"The 1485 battle in which Henry Tudor defeated Richard III","B":"Henry based his right partly on victory, marriage and descent through the Beaufort line","C":"The danger that disputed inheritance or weak legitimacy could destabilise the monarchy","D":"Henry inherited the throne in 1509 with a secure dynastic position and popular expectations"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-HIST-TUDORCLAIM","scaffold":"Henry VII's claim: Henry based his right partly on victory, marriage and descent through the Beaufort line. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"A student applies Henry VII's claim to a case. Which judgement is strongest?","options":{"A":"His rebellion exposed the continued vulnerability of Henry VII's position","B":"It makes dynastic security a key test of Tudor strength","C":"It created the foundation of Tudor rule but did not eliminate Yorkist opposition","D":"His insecure claim encouraged caution, surveillance and financial control"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-nea-content"},{"id":"HIST-TUDOR-03","spec":"AQA-NEA-TUDOR","stem":"A student is revising Battle of Bosworth. Which description is accurate?","options":{"A":"Henry based his right partly on victory, marriage and descent through the Beaufort line","B":"A pretender presented as the Earl of Warwick and supported by Yorkist opponents","C":"The 1485 battle in which Henry Tudor defeated Richard III","D":"A pretender claiming to be Richard of Shrewsbury who gained foreign support"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-HIST-TUDORCLAIM","scaffold":"Battle of Bosworth: The 1485 battle in which Henry Tudor defeated Richard III. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"What is the main significance of Battle of Bosworth?","options":{"A":"It created the foundation of Tudor rule but did not eliminate Yorkist opposition","B":"His insecure claim encouraged caution, surveillance and financial control","C":"His rebellion exposed the continued vulnerability of Henry VII's position","D":"Its display of magnificence supported prestige but produced little lasting strategic gain"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-nea-content"},{"id":"HIST-TUDOR-04","spec":"AQA-NEA-TUDOR","stem":"Which option about the two pretenders is correct?","options":{"A":"Simnel claimed to be the Earl of Warwick and was pardoned after Stoke Field","B":"Simnel claimed to be Richard of Shrewsbury and was executed in 1499","C":"Warbeck claimed to be the Earl of Warwick and ended his days in the royal kitchens","D":"Warbeck claimed to be Edward V and was pardoned by Henry VII after Bosworth"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-HIST-PRETENDERS","scaffold":"Simnel: claimed to be Edward, Earl of Warwick; active 1486–87; backed by Lincoln and Margaret of Burgundy with some 2,000 German mercenaries; defeated at Stoke Field, June 1487; PARDONED and put to work in the royal kitchens, later a falconer. Warbeck: claimed to be Richard of Shrewsbury, the younger Prince in the Tower; active 1491–99; backed at various points by Burgundy, France, Maximilian and James IV; captured 1497 and EXECUTED 1499. Memory hook: Simnel to the kitchens, Warbeck to the scaffold.","reforge":{"stem":"Why did Henry VII treat Simnel so leniently?","options":{"A":"Simnel had powerful family connections Henry could not afford to offend","B":"Simnel had surrendered voluntarily before the fighting began at Stoke","C":"Making a crowned pretender a kitchen boy made it absurd","D":"Executing a child would have breached the terms of the Treaty of Etaples"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-nea-content"},{"id":"HIST-TUDOR-05","spec":"AQA-NEA-TUDOR","stem":"Which example best illustrates Perkin Warbeck?","options":{"A":"A pretender claiming to be Richard of Shrewsbury who gained foreign support","B":"The use of dynastic marriages to improve legitimacy and foreign relations","C":"A pretender presented as the Earl of Warwick and supported by Yorkist opponents","D":"The use of crown lands, customs, feudal dues and fines to increase royal income"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-HIST-PRETENDERS","scaffold":"Perkin Warbeck: A pretender claiming to be Richard of Shrewsbury who gained foreign support. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"What is the defensible view of Perkin Warbeck?","options":{"A":"It strengthened security when combined with military and financial policy","B":"His rebellion exposed the continued vulnerability of Henry VII's position","C":"His long challenge tested Henry's diplomacy and security policies","D":"They increased royal control but created resentment and were unpopular"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-nea-content"},{"id":"HIST-TUDOR-06","spec":"AQA-NEA-TUDOR","stem":"What role does Tudor marriage diplomacy play in the topic?","options":{"A":"Financial guarantees imposed on nobles to enforce obedience","B":"The use of dynastic marriages to improve legitimacy and foreign relations","C":"A pretender claiming to be Richard of Shrewsbury who gained foreign support","D":"The use of crown lands, customs, feudal dues and fines to increase royal income"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-HIST-TUDORCLAIM","scaffold":"Tudor marriage diplomacy: The use of dynastic marriages to improve legitimacy and foreign relations. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"In a comparison question, what should a student say about Tudor marriage diplomacy?","options":{"A":"Its display of magnificence supported prestige but produced little lasting strategic gain","B":"His long challenge tested Henry's diplomacy and security policies","C":"They increased royal control but created resentment and were unpopular","D":"It strengthened security when combined with military and financial policy"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-nea-content"},{"id":"HIST-TUDOR-07","spec":"AQA-NEA-TUDOR","stem":"Why is Henry VII's bonds and recognisances significant?","options":{"A":"The use of dynastic marriages to improve legitimacy and foreign relations","B":"The use of crown lands, customs, feudal dues and fines to increase royal income","C":"Financial guarantees imposed on nobles to enforce obedience","D":"Henry inherited the throne in 1509 with a secure dynastic position and popular expectations"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-HIST-TUDOR-7","scaffold":"Henry VII's bonds and recognisances: Financial guarantees imposed on nobles to enforce obedience. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"Which outcome is most closely connected with Henry VII's bonds and recognisances?","options":{"A":"They increased royal control but created resentment and were unpopular","B":"It strengthened security when combined with military and financial policy","C":"It made the monarchy more solvent but could undermine consent","D":"The contrast with his father shows how legitimacy could change quickly"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-nea-content"},{"id":"HIST-TUDOR-08","spec":"AQA-NEA-TUDOR","stem":"What is the supported by the evidence view of Henry VII's financial policy?","options":{"A":"Thomas Wolsey became Henry VIII's chief minister and controlled much government business","B":"Henry inherited the throne in 1509 with a secure dynastic position and popular expectations","C":"Financial guarantees imposed on nobles to enforce obedience","D":"The use of crown lands, customs, feudal dues and fines to increase royal income"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-HIST-TUDOR-8","scaffold":"Henry VII's financial policy: The use of crown lands, customs, feudal dues and fines to increase royal income. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"A historian or political analyst considers Henry VII's financial policy. Which conclusion is sound?","options":{"A":"They increased royal control but created resentment and were unpopular","B":"It made the monarchy more solvent but could undermine consent","C":"The contrast with his father shows how legitimacy could change quickly","D":"Its display of magnificence supported prestige but produced little lasting strategic gain"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-nea-content"},{"id":"HIST-TUDOR-09","spec":"AQA-NEA-TUDOR","stem":"How does Henry VIII's accession affect the system or period being studied?","options":{"A":"Henry inherited the throne in 1509 with a secure dynastic position and popular expectations","B":"The 1520 diplomatic meeting between Henry VIII and Francis I","C":"The use of crown lands, customs, feudal dues and fines to increase royal income","D":"Henry VIII's rejection of papal authority and establishment of royal control over the English Church"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-HIST-TUDOR-9","scaffold":"Henry VIII's accession: Henry inherited the throne in 1509 with a secure dynastic position and popular expectations. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"Why might Henry VIII's accession matter in this context?","options":{"A":"Its display of magnificence supported prestige but produced little lasting strategic gain","B":"It made the monarchy more solvent but could undermine consent","C":"The contrast with his father shows how legitimacy could change quickly","D":"His influence depended on royal favour and failure to secure the annulment led to collapse"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-nea-content"},{"id":"HIST-TUDOR-10","spec":"AQA-NEA-TUDOR","stem":"Which distinction helps identify Field of Cloth of Gold?","options":{"A":"Thomas Wolsey became Henry VIII's chief minister and controlled much government business","B":"The 1520 diplomatic meeting between Henry VIII and Francis I","C":"Henry inherited the throne in 1509 with a secure dynastic position and popular expectations","D":"Attempts to improve legal access and administrative efficiency"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-HIST-TUDOR-10","scaffold":"Field of Cloth of Gold: The 1520 diplomatic meeting between Henry VIII and Francis I. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"Which interpretation of Field of Cloth of Gold is most accurate?","options":{"A":"They show that Wolsey was more than a foreign-policy minister, although results were limited","B":"The contrast with his father shows how legitimacy could change quickly","C":"His influence depended on royal favour and failure to secure the annulment led to collapse","D":"Its display of magnificence supported prestige but produced little lasting strategic gain"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-nea-content"},{"id":"HIST-TUDOR-11","spec":"AQA-NEA-TUDOR","stem":"What does the manner of Wolsey's and Cromwell's falls reveal about their power?","options":{"A":"That both had built landed affinities strong enough to threaten the Crown itself","B":"That it rested wholly on royal favour, and neither could mobilise anything to save himself","C":"That Parliament had grown strong enough by the 1530s to remove the king's chief ministers","D":"That the nobility controlled appointments and could dismiss ministers of low birth at will"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-HIST-MINISTERS","scaffold":"Wolsey: Lord Chancellor 1515, papal legate 1518, controlling church and state patronage; fell in 1529 over the annulment, arrested 1530, died at Leicester. Cromwell: Vicegerent in Spirituals, architect of the break, the Supremacy and the dissolution; fell over the Anne of Cleves marriage, attainted WITHOUT TRIAL, executed July 1540. Both fell within months of one personal failure. Neither had a landed following — a butcher's son and a cloth-worker's son — which is precisely why Henry raised them.","reforge":{"stem":"Why does the manner of Cromwell's fall complicate Elton's 'Tudor revolution in government'?","options":{"A":"Because Cromwell's administrative reforms were all reversed immediately after 1540","B":"Because Elton argued that Wolsey, not Cromwell, had designed the new machinery","C":"Because the reforms were introduced by Parliament rather than by Cromwell himself","D":"Because a bureaucratic state is odd work for a man a king could remove"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-nea-content"},{"id":"HIST-TUDOR-12","spec":"AQA-NEA-TUDOR","stem":"Which consequence can be linked to Wolsey's domestic reforms?","options":{"A":"Henry VIII's rejection of papal authority and establishment of royal control over the English Church","B":"A proposed non-parliamentary tax in 1525 that provoked resistance","C":"Thomas Wolsey became Henry VIII's chief minister and controlled much government business","D":"Attempts to improve legal access and administrative efficiency"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-HIST-MINISTERS","scaffold":"Wolsey's domestic reforms: Attempts to improve legal access and administrative efficiency. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"Which piece of evidence would best support an argument about Wolsey's domestic reforms?","options":{"A":"His influence depended on royal favour and failure to secure the annulment led to collapse","B":"They show that Wolsey was more than a foreign-policy minister, although results were limited","C":"Its failure revealed the limits of financial demands and contributed to Wolsey's fall","D":"His success depended on solving royal problems but his fall showed the danger of losing favour"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-nea-content"},{"id":"HIST-TUDOR-13","spec":"AQA-NEA-TUDOR","stem":"What does Amicable Grant refer to?","options":{"A":"A proposed non-parliamentary tax in 1525 that provoked resistance","B":"Henry VIII's rejection of papal authority and establishment of royal control over the English Church","C":"Attempts to improve legal access and administrative efficiency","D":"The law recognising Henry VIII as Supreme Head of the Church of England"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-HIST-MINISTERS","scaffold":"Amicable Grant: A proposed non-parliamentary tax in 1525 that provoked resistance. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"Which example demonstrates Amicable Grant?","options":{"A":"It transformed religion, government and property while originating in a dynastic problem","B":"They show that Wolsey was more than a foreign-policy minister, although results were limited","C":"Its failure revealed the limits of financial demands and contributed to Wolsey's fall","D":"It showed the depth of popular and elite resistance but lacked sustained coordination"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-nea-content"},{"id":"HIST-TUDOR-14","spec":"AQA-NEA-TUDOR","stem":"Was England Protestant in doctrine when Henry VIII died in 1547?","options":{"A":"Yes — the Act of Supremacy had established Protestant doctrine throughout the Church","B":"Yes — the dissolution of the monasteries had removed Catholic practice from England","C":"No — the Six Articles reaffirmed Catholic doctrine on pain of death in 1539","D":"No — Henry had reconciled with Rome and restored papal authority before his death"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-HIST-BREAK","scaffold":"Henry's break was about AUTHORITY, not belief. The Act in Restraint of Appeals and the Act of Supremacy transferred jurisdiction from Pope to King. Doctrine remained substantially Catholic: the Six Articles (1539) reaffirmed transubstantiation, clerical celibacy and private masses, and made denial punishable by death — the 'whip with six strings'. Henry died hearing mass. Doctrinal Protestantism arrives under Edward VI with the Prayer Books of 1549 and 1552.","reforge":{"stem":"Which measure of Henry VIII's reign was genuinely Protestant in character?","options":{"A":"The Act of Supremacy, which made the king Supreme Head of the Church","B":"The authorisation of an English Bible for use in every parish church","C":"The Act in Restraint of Appeals, which ended appeals to Rome","D":"The dissolution of the monasteries and the sale of their lands"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-nea-content"},{"id":"HIST-TUDOR-15","spec":"AQA-NEA-TUDOR","stem":"A student is revising Act of Supremacy 1534. Which description is accurate?","options":{"A":"Henry VIII's rejection of papal authority and establishment of royal control over the English Church","B":"The closure and transfer of monastic property to the Crown and its supporters","C":"The law recognising Henry VIII as Supreme Head of the Church of England","D":"The large 1536 rebellion in northern England against religious and governmental change"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-HIST-BREAK","scaffold":"Act of Supremacy 1534: The law recognising Henry VIII as Supreme Head of the Church of England. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"What is the main significance of Act of Supremacy 1534?","options":{"A":"It established the constitutional basis of the English Reformation","B":"It transformed religion, government and property while originating in a dynastic problem","C":"It increased royal resources and patronage but provoked resentment and rebellion","D":"It showed the depth of popular and elite resistance but lacked sustained coordination"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-nea-content"},{"id":"HIST-TUDOR-16","spec":"AQA-NEA-TUDOR","stem":"The Valor Ecclesiasticus valued monastic income at about £160,000 a year against Crown revenue of about £100,000. What does this suggest?","options":{"A":"That the monasteries were too poor to be worth the trouble of dissolving them","B":"That the visitations were right to report widespread corruption in the houses","C":"That Cromwell needed parliamentary approval before he could act on the survey","D":"That the financial motive for the dissolution needs no further explanation"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-HIST-DISSOLUTION","scaffold":"Monastic income exceeded the Crown's entire ordinary revenue by more than half again — the largest transfer of property in English history before the twentieth century. The visitation reports of corruption supplied the PRETEXT; the survey supplies the motive. Note the sequel: the land was SOLD, mostly to gentry, to fund the wars of the 1540s, converting a permanent income into a one-off windfall.","reforge":{"stem":"Why did the sale of monastic land make the Reformation harder to reverse?","options":{"A":"Because the monasteries were physically demolished and could not be rebuilt","B":"Because the proceeds were spent on wars and could not be repaid to Rome","C":"Because the Crown had no legal power to reclaim land once it had been sold","D":"Because thousands of gentry now had a stake in it lasting"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-nea-content"},{"id":"HIST-TUDOR-17","spec":"AQA-NEA-TUDOR","stem":"What did the Pilgrimage of Grace demand?","options":{"A":"An end to the dissolution and removal of evil counsellors","B":"The deposition of Henry VIII and his replacement by Mary Tudor","C":"The abolition of enclosure and reform of corrupt local government","D":"The restoration of papal authority and a Spanish marriage for the king"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-HIST-REBELLION","scaffold":"The Pontefract Articles profess loyalty to Henry and demand the removal of advisers — chiefly Cromwell — alongside religious and taxation grievances. That is the standard Tudor pattern: rebels claimed loyalty and attacked counsellors, because attacking the monarch directly was treason and lost the waverers. Of the major risings only the Northern Rebellion of 1569 genuinely sought to replace the monarch. Kett (1549) is the trap in any list: its 29 articles are about enclosure, barely religious at all.","reforge":{"stem":"Which rebellion in the list genuinely aimed at replacing the reigning monarch?","options":{"A":"Kett's Rebellion of 1549, which sought a new regime in East Anglia","B":"The Western Rebellion of 1549, which demanded the Latin mass be restored","C":"The Northern Rebellion of 1569, seeking Mary Stuart's accession","D":"The Pilgrimage of Grace of 1536, which marched south to depose Henry VIII"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-nea-content"},{"id":"HIST-TUDOR-18","spec":"AQA-NEA-TUDOR","stem":"What role does Thomas Cromwell play in the topic?","options":{"A":"The 1539 law reaffirming several traditional Catholic doctrines","B":"Henry VIII's minister who reorganised government and managed the Reformation","C":"The large 1536 rebellion in northern England against religious and governmental change","D":"Legislation altering the order of succession and legitimising or excluding his children"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-HIST-MINISTERS","scaffold":"Thomas Cromwell: Henry VIII's minister who reorganised government and managed the Reformation. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"In a comparison question, what should a student say about Thomas Cromwell?","options":{"A":"His attempt to place Lady Jane Grey on the throne failed because legitimacy and support were weak","B":"It showed the depth of popular and elite resistance but lacked sustained coordination","C":"The Reformation was politically driven, not consistently Protestant","D":"His success depended on solving royal problems but his fall showed the danger of losing favour"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-nea-content"},{"id":"HIST-TUDOR-19","spec":"AQA-NEA-TUDOR","stem":"Why is Act of Six Articles significant?","options":{"A":"Henry VIII's minister who reorganised government and managed the Reformation","B":"Legislation altering the order of succession and legitimising or excluding his children","C":"The 1539 law reaffirming several traditional Catholic doctrines","D":"The reign of Edward VI was dominated by competing regents and religious reformers"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-HIST-BREAK","scaffold":"Act of Six Articles: The 1539 law reaffirming several traditional Catholic doctrines. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"Which outcome is most closely connected with Act of Six Articles?","options":{"A":"The Reformation was politically driven, not consistently Protestant","B":"His success depended on solving royal problems but his fall showed the danger of losing favour","C":"It made parliament central to dynastic security and reflected uncertainty over heirs","D":"It reduced direct royal control and made faction especially important"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-nea-content"},{"id":"HIST-TUDOR-20","spec":"AQA-NEA-TUDOR","stem":"What is the supported by the evidence view of Henry VIII's succession acts?","options":{"A":"John Dudley dominated government after Somerset's fall and promoted a Protestant succession","B":"The reign of Edward VI was dominated by competing regents and religious reformers","C":"The 1539 law reaffirming several traditional Catholic doctrines","D":"Legislation altering the order of succession and legitimising or excluding his children"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-HIST-TUDORCLAIM","scaffold":"Henry VIII's succession acts: Legislation altering the order of succession and legitimising or excluding his children. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"A historian or political analyst considers Henry VIII's succession acts. Which conclusion is sound?","options":{"A":"The Reformation was politically driven, not consistently Protestant","B":"It made parliament central to dynastic security and reflected uncertainty over heirs","C":"It reduced direct royal control and made faction especially important","D":"His attempt to place Lady Jane Grey on the throne failed because legitimacy and support were weak"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-nea-content"},{"id":"HIST-TUDOR-21","spec":"AQA-NEA-TUDOR","stem":"How does Edward VI's minority affect the system or period being studied?","options":{"A":"The reign of Edward VI was dominated by competing regents and religious reformers","B":"Edward Seymour governed as Lord Protector during the early years of Edward VI","C":"Legislation altering the order of succession and legitimising or excluding his children","D":"John Dudley dominated government after Somerset's fall and promoted a Protestant succession"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-HIST-TUDOR-21","scaffold":"Edward VI's minority: The reign of Edward VI was dominated by competing regents and religious reformers. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"Why might Edward VI's minority matter in this context?","options":{"A":"His social and military policies created opposition and contributed to his fall","B":"It made parliament central to dynastic security and reflected uncertainty over heirs","C":"It reduced direct royal control and made faction especially important","D":"It revealed tensions within reforming government and the limits of central control"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-nea-content"},{"id":"HIST-TUDOR-22","spec":"AQA-NEA-TUDOR","stem":"Which distinction helps identify Somerset's rule?","options":{"A":"A 1549 East Anglian rebellion focused on enclosure and local grievances","B":"Edward Seymour governed as Lord Protector during the early years of Edward VI","C":"The reign of Edward VI was dominated by competing regents and religious reformers","D":"John Dudley dominated government after Somerset's fall and promoted a Protestant succession"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-HIST-TUDOR-22","scaffold":"Somerset's rule: Edward Seymour governed as Lord Protector during the early years of Edward VI. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"Which interpretation of Somerset's rule is most accurate?","options":{"A":"His attempt to place Lady Jane Grey on the throne failed because legitimacy and support were weak","B":"It reduced direct royal control and made faction especially important","C":"It revealed tensions within reforming government and the limits of central control","D":"His social and military policies created opposition and contributed to his fall"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-nea-content"},{"id":"HIST-TUDOR-23","spec":"AQA-NEA-TUDOR","stem":"A teacher asks for an accurate account of Kett's Rebellion. Which answer works?","options":{"A":"Edward Seymour governed as Lord Protector during the early years of Edward VI","B":"John Dudley dominated government after Somerset's fall and promoted a Protestant succession","C":"A 1549 East Anglian rebellion focused on enclosure and local grievances","D":"Mary gained the throne in 1553 with broad support against the attempt to exclude her"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-HIST-REBELLION","scaffold":"Kett's Rebellion: A 1549 East Anglian rebellion focused on enclosure and local grievances. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"What does a case involving Kett's Rebellion show?","options":{"A":"It revealed tensions within reforming government and the limits of central control","B":"His social and military policies created opposition and contributed to his fall","C":"His attempt to place Lady Jane Grey on the throne failed because legitimacy and support were weak","D":"Her success demonstrated that gender did not make rule impossible but did create anxieties"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-nea-content"},{"id":"HIST-TUDOR-24","spec":"AQA-NEA-TUDOR","stem":"Which consequence can be linked to Northumberland's rule?","options":{"A":"The 1559 religious settlement established a Protestant church with compromises in doctrine and worship","B":"The Protestant claimant placed on the throne for nine days in 1553","C":"A 1549 East Anglian rebellion focused on enclosure and local grievances","D":"John Dudley dominated government after Somerset's fall and promoted a Protestant succession"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-HIST-TUDOR-24","scaffold":"Northumberland's rule: John Dudley dominated government after Somerset's fall and promoted a Protestant succession. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"Which piece of evidence would best support an argument about Northumberland's rule?","options":{"A":"It revealed tensions within reforming government and the limits of central control","B":"His attempt to place Lady Jane Grey on the throne failed because legitimacy and support were weak","C":"Her failure shows the importance of dynastic legitimacy and popular support","D":"Its defeat strengthened Elizabeth's image but was aided by strategy, weather and Spanish problems"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-nea-content"},{"id":"HIST-TUDOR-25","spec":"AQA-NEA-TUDOR","stem":"What does Lady Jane Grey refer to?","options":{"A":"The Protestant claimant placed on the throne for nine days in 1553","B":"Mary gained the throne in 1553 with broad support against the attempt to exclude her","C":"John Dudley dominated government after Somerset's fall and promoted a Protestant succession","D":"The Spanish marriage intended to secure a Catholic alliance and a possible heir"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-HIST-TUDORCLAIM","scaffold":"Lady Jane Grey: The Protestant claimant placed on the throne for nine days in 1553. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"Which example demonstrates Lady Jane Grey?","options":{"A":"Her success demonstrated that gender did not make rule impossible but did create anxieties","B":"His attempt to place Lady Jane Grey on the throne failed because legitimacy and support were weak","C":"Her failure shows the importance of dynastic legitimacy and popular support","D":"It was legally significant but lacked time and broad enough consent to become secure"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-nea-content"},{"id":"HIST-TUDOR-26","spec":"AQA-NEA-TUDOR","stem":"Which feature is most closely associated with Mary I's accession?","options":{"A":"Mary attempted to restore Catholic worship and papal authority","B":"Mary gained the throne in 1553 with broad support against the attempt to exclude her","C":"The Protestant claimant placed on the throne for nine days in 1553","D":"The 1559 religious settlement established a Protestant church with compromises in doctrine and worship"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-HIST-TUDORCLAIM","scaffold":"Mary I's accession: Mary gained the throne in 1553 with broad support against the attempt to exclude her. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"A student applies Mary I's accession to a case. Which judgement is strongest?","options":{"A":"Its defeat strengthened Elizabeth's image but was aided by strategy, weather and Spanish problems","B":"Her failure shows the importance of dynastic legitimacy and popular support","C":"It was legally significant but lacked time and broad enough consent to become secure","D":"Her success demonstrated that gender did not make rule impossible but did create anxieties"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-nea-content"},{"id":"HIST-TUDOR-27","spec":"AQA-NEA-TUDOR","stem":"A student is revising Mary I's religious restoration. Which description is accurate?","options":{"A":"Mary gained the throne in 1553 with broad support against the attempt to exclude her","B":"The execution of Protestant heretics during Mary's reign","C":"Mary attempted to restore Catholic worship and papal authority","D":"The Spanish marriage intended to secure a Catholic alliance and a possible heir"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-HIST-TUDOR-27","scaffold":"Mary I's religious restoration: Mary attempted to restore Catholic worship and papal authority. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"What is the main significance of Mary I's religious restoration?","options":{"A":"It was legally significant but lacked time and broad enough consent to become secure","B":"Her success demonstrated that gender did not make rule impossible but did create anxieties","C":"They damaged Mary's reputation and made religious policy a central issue of succession","D":"Its failure strengthened government control but encouraged harsher anti-Catholic laws"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-nea-content"},{"id":"HIST-TUDOR-28","spec":"AQA-NEA-TUDOR","stem":"How should Marian persecutions be defined in this course?","options":{"A":"The 1559 religious settlement established a Protestant church with compromises in doctrine and worship","B":"The Spanish marriage intended to secure a Catholic alliance and a possible heir","C":"Mary attempted to restore Catholic worship and papal authority","D":"The execution of Protestant heretics during Mary's reign"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-HIST-TUDOR-28","scaffold":"Marian persecutions: The execution of Protestant heretics during Mary's reign. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"How should Marian persecutions be evaluated?","options":{"A":"It was legally significant but lacked time and broad enough consent to become secure","B":"They damaged Mary's reputation and made religious policy a central issue of succession","C":"It provoked fears of foreign influence and did not produce the desired succession","D":"Its defeat strengthened Elizabeth's image but was aided by strategy, weather and Spanish problems"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-nea-content"},{"id":"HIST-TUDOR-29","spec":"AQA-NEA-TUDOR","stem":"Which example best illustrates Mary I's marriage to Philip II?","options":{"A":"The Spanish marriage intended to secure a Catholic alliance and a possible heir","B":"The 1559 religious settlement established a Protestant church with compromises in doctrine and worship","C":"The execution of Protestant heretics during Mary's reign","D":"The refusal of Catholics to attend the established church or accept its authority"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-HIST-TUDOR-29","scaffold":"Mary I's marriage to Philip II: The Spanish marriage intended to secure a Catholic alliance and a possible heir. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"What is the defensible view of Mary I's marriage to Philip II?","options":{"A":"It prioritised political stability and avoided the most extreme alternatives","B":"They damaged Mary's reputation and made religious policy a central issue of succession","C":"It provoked fears of foreign influence and did not produce the desired succession","D":"It became more politically threatening after missionary activity and foreign plots"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-nea-content"},{"id":"HIST-TUDOR-30","spec":"AQA-NEA-TUDOR","stem":"Why is 'settlement' a questionable description of the religious legislation of 1559?","options":{"A":"Because it was repealed within a decade and replaced by a stricter code","B":"Because it provoked Puritan pressure and Catholic recusancy for forty years","C":"Because Parliament rejected the Act of Uniformity and passed only the Supremacy","D":"Because Elizabeth herself refused to enforce any part of it during her reign"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-HIST-ELIZSETTLE","scaffold":"The Act of Supremacy made Elizabeth Supreme GOVERNOR rather than Head, and the Act of Uniformity imposed the 1559 Prayer Book with a 12d recusancy fine. The communion wording deliberately spliced the 1549 and 1552 forms so it could be read either way. What followed: the Vestiarian controversy from 1566, the Admonition of 1572, the Northern Rebellion of 1569, Regnans in Excelsis in 1570, and penalties escalating to £20 a month in 1581 and treason for priesthood in 1585.","reforge":{"stem":"What was the purpose of the ambiguous communion wording in the 1559 Prayer Book?","options":{"A":"To conceal from Rome that England had adopted a Protestant liturgy","B":"To allow either a real presence or a memorial reading","C":"To leave the doctrine to be settled by a later church council","D":"To reproduce the wording of the Latin mass as closely as possible"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-nea-content"},{"id":"HIST-TUDOR-31","spec":"AQA-NEA-TUDOR","stem":"Why is Act of Uniformity 1559 significant?","options":{"A":"The 1559 religious settlement established a Protestant church with compromises in doctrine and worship","B":"Pressure from more radical Protestants for further reform of the Church","C":"The law requiring a common form of worship in the Church of England","D":"The refusal of Catholics to attend the established church or accept its authority"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-HIST-ELIZSETTLE","scaffold":"Act of Uniformity 1559: The law requiring a common form of worship in the Church of England. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"Which outcome is most closely connected with Act of Uniformity 1559?","options":{"A":"It made religious conformity a matter of law while allowing some ambiguity","B":"It prioritised political stability and avoided the most extreme alternatives","C":"It became a long-term test of Elizabeth's control over religious debate","D":"It became more politically threatening after missionary activity and foreign plots"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-nea-content"},{"id":"HIST-TUDOR-32","spec":"AQA-NEA-TUDOR","stem":"What is the supported by the evidence view of Puritan challenge?","options":{"A":"A Catholic rising seeking to alter Elizabeth's religious and dynastic settlement","B":"The refusal of Catholics to attend the established church or accept its authority","C":"The law requiring a common form of worship in the Church of England","D":"Pressure from more radical Protestants for further reform of the Church"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-HIST-ELIZSETTLE","scaffold":"Puritan challenge: Pressure from more radical Protestants for further reform of the Church. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"A historian or political analyst considers Puritan challenge. Which conclusion is sound?","options":{"A":"It made religious conformity a matter of law while allowing some ambiguity","B":"It became a long-term test of Elizabeth's control over religious debate","C":"It became more politically threatening after missionary activity and foreign plots","D":"Its failure strengthened government control but encouraged harsher anti-Catholic laws"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-nea-content"},{"id":"HIST-TUDOR-33","spec":"AQA-NEA-TUDOR","stem":"How does Catholic recusancy affect the system or period being studied?","options":{"A":"The refusal of Catholics to attend the established church or accept its authority","B":"A Catholic rising seeking to alter Elizabeth's religious and dynastic settlement","C":"Pressure from more radical Protestants for further reform of the Church","D":"Tudor rulers were strongest when legitimacy, finance, religion and patronage worked together"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-HIST-ELIZSETTLE","scaffold":"Catholic recusancy: The refusal of Catholics to attend the established church or accept its authority. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"Why might Catholic recusancy matter in this context?","options":{"A":"Its failure strengthened government control but encouraged harsher anti-Catholic laws","B":"It became a long-term test of Elizabeth's control over religious debate","C":"It became more politically threatening after missionary activity and foreign plots","D":"Its defeat strengthened Elizabeth's image but was aided by strategy, weather and Spanish problems"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-nea-content"},{"id":"HIST-TUDOR-34","spec":"AQA-NEA-TUDOR","stem":"Which distinction helps identify Northern Rebellion 1569?","options":{"A":"A Catholic conspiracy involving Mary Queen of Scots and foreign support","B":"A Catholic rising seeking to alter Elizabeth's religious and dynastic settlement","C":"The refusal of Catholics to attend the established church or accept its authority","D":"Tudor rulers were strongest when legitimacy, finance, religion and patronage worked together"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-HIST-REBELLION","scaffold":"Northern Rebellion 1569: A Catholic rising seeking to alter Elizabeth's religious and dynastic settlement. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"Which interpretation of Northern Rebellion 1569 is most accurate?","options":{"A":"Its defeat strengthened Elizabeth's image but was aided by strategy, weather and Spanish problems","B":"It became more politically threatening after missionary activity and foreign plots","C":"It reinforced fears that religious dissent could become treason","D":"Its failure strengthened government control but encouraged harsher anti-Catholic laws"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-nea-content"},{"id":"HIST-TUDOR-35","spec":"AQA-NEA-TUDOR","stem":"A teacher asks for an accurate account of Ridolfi Plot. Which answer works?","options":{"A":"A Catholic rising seeking to alter Elizabeth's religious and dynastic settlement","B":"The failed Spanish attempt to escort an invasion force to England","C":"A Catholic conspiracy involving Mary Queen of Scots and foreign support","D":"Local and national measures culminating in the 1601 Poor Law to address poverty"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-HIST-REBELLION","scaffold":"Ridolfi Plot: A Catholic conspiracy involving Mary Queen of Scots and foreign support. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"What does a case involving Ridolfi Plot show?","options":{"A":"It reinforced fears that religious dissent could become treason","B":"Its failure strengthened government control but encouraged harsher anti-Catholic laws","C":"Its defeat strengthened Elizabeth's image but was aided by strategy, weather and Spanish problems","D":"It shows the growth of state regulation while distinguishing the deserving from the idle poor"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-nea-content"},{"id":"HIST-TUDOR-36","spec":"AQA-NEA-TUDOR","stem":"Which consequence can be linked to Spanish Armada 1588?","options":{"A":"A small group of advisers and administrators who helped the monarch govern","B":"Local and national measures culminating in the 1601 Poor Law to address poverty","C":"A Catholic conspiracy involving Mary Queen of Scots and foreign support","D":"The failed Spanish attempt to escort an invasion force to England"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-HIST-TUDOR-36","scaffold":"Spanish Armada 1588: The failed Spanish attempt to escort an invasion force to England. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"Which piece of evidence would best support an argument about Spanish Armada 1588?","options":{"A":"It reinforced fears that religious dissent could become treason","B":"Its defeat strengthened Elizabeth's image but was aided by strategy, weather and Spanish problems","C":"It shows the growth of state regulation while distinguishing the deserving from the idle poor","D":"A historical investigation should compare individual monarchs and recognise limits to royal power"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-nea-content"},{"id":"HIST-TUDOR-37","spec":"AQA-NEA-TUDOR","stem":"What does Elizabethan poor relief refer to?","options":{"A":"Local and national measures culminating in the 1601 Poor Law to address poverty","B":"Parliament was needed for taxation; the Council ran policy","C":"The failed Spanish attempt to escort an invasion force to England","D":"Tudor rulers were strongest when legitimacy, finance, religion and patronage worked together"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-HIST-TUDOR-37","scaffold":"Elizabethan poor relief: Local and national measures culminating in the 1601 Poor Law to address poverty. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"Which example demonstrates Elizabethan poor relief?","options":{"A":"It demonstrates strong monarchy rather than absolute rule","B":"A permanent increase in the ordinary revenue available to the Crown","C":"It shows the growth of state regulation while distinguishing the deserving from the idle poor","D":"A historical investigation should compare individual monarchs and recognise limits to royal power"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-nea-content"},{"id":"HIST-TUDOR-38","spec":"AQA-NEA-TUDOR","stem":"Which feature is most closely associated with Tudor government and Parliament?","options":{"A":"A small group of advisers and administrators who helped the monarch govern","B":"Parliament was needed for taxation; the Council ran policy","C":"Local and national measures culminating in the 1601 Poor Law to address poverty","D":"Tudor rulers were strongest when legitimacy, finance, religion and patronage worked together"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-HIST-TUDOR-38","scaffold":"Tudor government and Parliament: Parliament was needed for taxation; the Council ran policy. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"A student applies Tudor government and Parliament to a case. Which judgement is strongest?","options":{"A":"A historical investigation should compare individual monarchs and recognise limits to royal power","B":"It shows the growth of state regulation while distinguishing the deserving from the idle poor","C":"Its growing importance allowed Tudor rulers to coordinate policy while managing faction","D":"It demonstrates strong monarchy rather than absolute rule"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-nea-content"},{"id":"HIST-TUDOR-39","spec":"AQA-NEA-TUDOR","stem":"A student is revising Privy Council. Which description is accurate?","options":{"A":"Parliament was needed for taxation; the Council ran policy","B":"Tudor rulers were strongest when legitimacy, finance, religion and patronage worked together","C":"A small group of advisers and administrators who helped the monarch govern","D":"The danger that disputed inheritance or weak legitimacy could destabilise the monarchy"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-HIST-TUDOR-39","scaffold":"Privy Council: A small group of advisers and administrators who helped the monarch govern. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"What is the main significance of Privy Council?","options":{"A":"Its growing importance allowed Tudor rulers to coordinate policy while managing faction","B":"It demonstrates strong monarchy rather than absolute rule","C":"A historical investigation should compare individual monarchs and recognise limits to royal power","D":"Its display of magnificence supported prestige but produced little lasting strategic gain"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-nea-content"},{"id":"HIST-TUDOR-40","spec":"AQA-NEA-TUDOR","stem":"How should Tudor monarchy judgement be defined in this course?","options":{"A":"Henry VIII's rejection of papal authority and establishment of royal control over the English Church","B":"The danger that disputed inheritance or weak legitimacy could destabilise the monarchy","C":"A small group of advisers and administrators who helped the monarch govern","D":"Tudor rulers were strongest when legitimacy, finance, religion and patronage worked together"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-HIST-JUDGEMENT","scaffold":"Tudor monarchy judgement: Tudor rulers were strongest when legitimacy, finance, religion and patronage worked together. In an A-level answer, connect the evidence to causation, change and continuity, significance, or the strength of a historical interpretation.","reforge":{"stem":"How should Tudor monarchy judgement be evaluated?","options":{"A":"Its growing importance allowed Tudor rulers to coordinate policy while managing faction","B":"A historical investigation should compare individual monarchs and recognise limits to royal power","C":"It makes dynastic security a key test of Tudor strength","D":"His attempt to place Lady Jane Grey on the throne failed because legitimacy and support were weak"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-nea-content"},{"id":"HIST-TUDOR-41","spec":"AQA-NEA-TUDOR","tag":"MC-HIST-DISSOLUTION","specPointId":"aqa-a-hist-nea-content","stem":"Why was the Crown no better off financially by 1547, despite the dissolution?","options":{"A":"Because the monastic income had been overstated by the 1535 survey","B":"Because Mary I returned most of the monastic land to the Church in 1553","C":"Because the former monks had to be paid pensions for the rest of their lives","D":"Because the land was sold to fund the wars of the 1540s rather than retained"},"correct":"D","scaffold":"Around 800 houses were dissolved between 1536 and 1540, transferring an income worth more than the Crown's entire ordinary revenue. But most of the land was SOLD, largely to gentry and nobility, to pay for Henry's French and Scottish wars — converting a permanent annual income into a one-off windfall that was spent. Pensions to former religious were a real but much smaller drain. The lasting consequence was political rather than financial: the buyers acquired a stake in the Reformation being permanent.","reforge":{"stem":"What was the most durable consequence of the dissolution?","options":{"A":"A permanent increase in the ordinary revenue available to the Crown","B":"The end of monasticism as a route to social advancement in England","C":"The transfer of educational provision from the Church to the state","D":"A landed class invested in the Reformation lasting"},"correct":"D"}}]}});
